{"items":[{"tags":["toddler","tantrums"],"owner":{"account_id":29913359,"reputation":21,"user_id":45282,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6de83757073a6fa7b12d2b9b7943dbb8?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Mk08","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45282/mk08"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":551,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1708257985,"creation_date":1704485185,"question_id":43901,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43901/3-year-old-tantrums","title":"3 year old tantrums","body":"<p>My now almost 3 year old daughter has been having some trouble in the last few weeks of needing help for things she usually does for herself. This includes things like getting into highchair/booster seat, changing clothes, getting down off her bed.</p>\n<p>This is coincided with some of her tantrums becoming more frequent(at least several times a week) along with becoming a bit longer. The occasional one will go beyond 10 mins. Just the other day she threw a massive tantrum/meltdown that lasted about 20 mins with an intermission in between because she could not get unstuck from the action she wanted to do. She is an otherwise healthy child who is very verbal, developing along all the milestones but does have an anxious side to her.</p>\n<p>Has this occurred to other families, and if so how were you able to assist your little one through that time?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","teen","blended-family"],"owner":{"account_id":30279477,"reputation":159,"user_id":45438,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/PukAf.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Kate","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45438/kate"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":415,"accepted_answer_id":43913,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1704708227,"creation_date":1704553454,"question_id":43908,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43908/older-child-11-years-jealous-of-2-year-old","title":"Older child 11 years jealous of 2 year old?","body":"<p>My daughter is 2 years old, she is girl. My son 11 years old is my step son, he is obviously raised by both, his mom and his dad (my partner). I have noticed recently, that he is jealous of my and his dad daughter. My daughter is my first born child and I have different approach to her, I see this too. I am more of a mom now, that I have my own child and my mom instincts than I had when my step son was her age. Have any one had any experience with this kind of situation?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","potty-training"],"owner":{"account_id":19798857,"reputation":153,"user_id":45440,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/DzFha.jpg?s=256","display_name":"E.Aigle","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45440/e-aigle"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":961,"accepted_answer_id":43918,"answer_count":2,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1705206643,"creation_date":1704623061,"question_id":43910,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43910/4-5-year-old-will-use-diaper-instead-of-toilet-because-its-not-a-big-deal","title":"4.5 year old will use diaper instead of toilet because &quot;it&#39;s not a big deal&quot;","body":"<p>My 4.5 year old step son, uses the toilet during the day for number 1 and 2, but still wets the bed. He is consequently still sleeping in diapers. This allows for a small time in the morning where he is awake, and in a diaper. If he needs to use the toilet during this time, he will make no effort to go to the toilet and just go in his diaper (poo or pee). We try to be as kind and understanding with night time accidents, but it seems he is understanding this &quot;no big deal&quot; attitude to mean that using a diaper if he has it on is perfectly fine.\nHow can we encourage him to use the toilet rather than his diaper at all times? We already have &quot;take your diaper off immediately when you wake up&quot; kind of rules in place but they often fall to the wayside if he gets distracted.</p>\n"},{"tags":["language-development","bilingual"],"owner":{"account_id":63513,"reputation":153,"user_id":45443,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/13a7fe2f6b6644b287d80fedae50dc3a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"jogloran","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45443/jogloran"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1038,"accepted_answer_id":43924,"answer_count":2,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1705592913,"creation_date":1704681721,"question_id":43912,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43912/when-is-it-appropriate-to-stop-one-parent-one-language","title":"When is it appropriate to stop &quot;one parent, one language&quot;?","body":"<p>Are there milestones that, once reached, might mean that we no longer have to do one-parent-one-language (OPOL) — that is, what's the point at which both parents can speak both languages to the child, without losing the benefits of OPOL?</p>\n"},{"tags":["infant","newborn","pacifier"],"owner":{"account_id":30293037,"reputation":33,"user_id":45445,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/bmn8U.jpg?s=256","display_name":"paulherr","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45445/paulherr"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":5431,"answer_count":5,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1709899274,"creation_date":1704744918,"question_id":43919,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43919/infant-needs-help-with-pacifier-falling-out-of-her-mouth-she-cant-keep-her-pac","title":"Infant needs help with pacifier falling out of her mouth, she can&#39;t keep her pacifier in her mouth. How to stop pacifier from falling out?","body":"<p>The real problem here. My hands are numb from putting a pacifier back in place all the time. My daughter (4 weeks old) seems not to be able to keep the pacifier in her mouth. She sucks it for some time, then she relaxes and suddenly pacifier falls out. The hell breaks loose then. She will not stop crying bloody murder unless I find the fallen-out pacifier and put it back in her mouth for her. I am watching her, so I know this is unintentional. She can't keep her pacifier in her mouth. This is causing lots and lots of distress for both, us and her. We are on the 15th different brand of pacifiers, none seems to sort the issue. she wants the pacifier, that's for sure. Some brands are better than others, but none solves the issue completely. Has anyone had a similar problem? How did you go about it? Either I or my wife, are stuck next to our baby daughter for hours, just putting the pacifier back in her mouth and hoping she can keep it longer this time. We tried pulling it slightly to improve her sucking but no luck so far.</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","pregnancy","anger"],"owner":{"account_id":1738871,"reputation":41,"user_id":45472,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/4a6a0c0ccefe99e676356fde347ed7b2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Romans","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45472/romans"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":421,"answer_count":1,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1722406521,"creation_date":1705491788,"question_id":43932,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43932/onset-of-aggression-towards-mother","title":"Onset of aggression towards mother","body":"<p>I am a mother with 4.5 year old daughter. I am also expecting a second child and am in the second trimester.\nEver since we shared the news with the daughter, she became much more needy. We talk a lot and treat with respect but nevertheless she needs me more. My husband had to stop reading books to her before sleep because she started rejecting this previously standard practice.</p>\n<p>Recently she, who is really calm, started to have anger issues that results in her hitting us with her fists. For example, when we say no to cartoons, she becomes visibly angry and becomes aggressive. We don't punish her for such actions, but ask her to stop hitting us and maybe consider going and drawing her feelings. She most often agrees and after drawing flowers or colouring something, says that she does not know what happened.</p>\n<p>A week back she did it in public for the first time leaving us startled.</p>\n<p>We think her feelings has something to do with the new baby, however we also cannot dismiss generic anger issues which my husband has (albeit, he dealt with it and my daughter has never seen him angry)</p>\n<p><strong>The ask:</strong></p>\n<p>What resources (books, studies) could I use to understand what is going on?\nHow to deal with it? We are against punishment apart from showing that we are upset because it is normal that she has a lot of emotions</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","safety","death"],"owner":{"account_id":13307205,"reputation":121,"user_id":43612,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/074b15c4bd736176af6b9199da622aa3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"A.Steer","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/43612/a-steer"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":522,"answer_count":1,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1705592141,"creation_date":1705581814,"question_id":43934,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43934/what-should-be-put-in-place-to-ensure-a-child-can-survive-if-both-parents-were-t","title":"What should be put in place to ensure a child can survive if both parents were to die in the home?","body":"<p>Recently in the UK the <a href=\"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-lincolnshire-68007571\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">death of a Toddler and his Father has come to light</a>.</p>\n<p>In this instance the father died of a heart attack and his 2 year old son died of (potentially) starvation several days later.</p>\n<p>The specific circumstances were an adult dies and the toddler is then unable to fend for themselves has been a fear of mine since the birth of my child.</p>\n<p>My circumstances are different in that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myself and my partner are in a commited relationship.</li>\n<li>My toddler attends a nursery so would hopefully be missed.</li>\n<li>Myself and my partner both work full time jobs, so would hopefully be missed.</li>\n<li>We have a support network of friends and family, who would <em>hopefully</em> be concerned if they haven't heard from us.</li>\n<li>We have no involvement with government or regulatory authorities in relation to our toddler.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>But the simple matter is if either one or both of us were to die in the house and our toddler was alone, what could I put in place now to prolong my toddlers life as long as possible.</p>\n<p>Currently my toddler is unable to reach most food stuffs as these are either kept above the reach of my toddler or are in a fridge that my toddler is unlikely to be able to open.</p>\n<p>Tap water would also be generally unobtainable due to the height of the taps. My toddler does have a kitchen stool that allows access to worktops, but it would never be guaranteed to be in a position close to food or water. There are likely to be one or two bottles of water available at toddler height around the house, which they do drink from when they see them.</p>\n<p>My toddler knows where to go to get food, to feed our cat (either dry food or wet food in sachets) and we have both cat food and water available at floor level. We do tell our toddler that this food is for our cat and not for them.</p>\n<p>Me and my partner both have mobile phones and we have no landline phone in the house. (We have a landline just no phone for it). Our toddler understands that our mobile phones are communication devices but I'm unsure they would know how to use them.</p>\n<p>What changes or additions could be made to prolong my toddlers life if the death of a parent was to happen within the home?</p>\n"},{"tags":["potty-training"],"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":399,"accepted_answer_id":43958,"answer_count":2,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1709114028,"creation_date":1706390910,"question_id":43945,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43945/were-taking-a-break-from-potty-training-whats-next","title":"We&#39;re taking a break from potty training - what&#39;s next?","body":"<p>A little background, we've had some difficulty potty training our 3.75 year old boy. He's definitely made some progress but generally isn't that self motivated to go by himself. We've tried a few different approaches, including rewards, but nothing seems to work. My wife has also had some communication issues that haven't helped.</p>\n<p>Finally we talked to our family doctor who recommended that we put him back in diapers for a couple months. And she said that where we're at is within the range of normal.</p>\n<p>My question is: how do we approach potty training again once we're done the break? What would re-introducing him to potty training actually look like, given that he's resistant to everything and will only go when we bring him to the toilet or prompt him?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","sleep"],"owner":{"account_id":30450783,"reputation":11,"user_id":45543,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9a89924dcdabe48676193e707a1da329?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Yio","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45543/yio"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":808,"answer_count":2,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1709845630,"creation_date":1706858796,"last_edit_date":1707134958,"question_id":43950,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43950/15-month-old-wakes-up-crying-at-night-and-wont-sleep-again-without-me","title":"15 month old wakes up crying at night and wont sleep again without me","body":"<p>My 15mo used to sleep all night ,until a month ago.He now wakes up at night crying and standing on his feet and wont sleep until i lay with him and sleep together. How do I stop this from happening?</p>\n<p>It is not teething (tried given him medicine but still woke up after 2 hours). He usually eats a snack around 19.00 and drinks a bottle around 20.00 and goes to sleep at 21.00.</p>\n<p>There is white noise and a little light, I've also tried no light but he still wakes up. His diaper is clean and temperature is 70 degrees fahreneit .</p>\n<p>This is happening every night , approximately 3 hours after he falls asleep. When he starts crying his eyes are open , i dont think it is nightmares .He was sleeptrained (pickup put down method) and knew how to selfsooth with a pacifier. When he starts crying , I pick him and hold him and he seems to fall asleep instantly but the minute I try to put him back in his crib ,starts crying again.i put a matress on the floor and lay down with him , and he stays asleep when when I am there with him. But he continues to try and find me in his sleep, wants to hold me or sleep on top of me.</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","independence"],"owner":{"account_id":1530716,"reputation":3476,"user_id":36371,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/48248a8939c6db9b3d90f02b72354198?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"stan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36371/stan"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1019,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1742329605,"creation_date":1707388109,"question_id":43956,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43956/how-can-i-help-my-5yo-feel-more-in-control-of-her-life","title":"How can I help my 5yo feel more in control of her life","body":"<p>I have an extremely smart, extremely independent 5 year old daughter. She's turning 6 in a couple of months.</p>\n<p>This year in particular, starting her reception year at school, she has a lot less free time to her disposal. We have a regular routine, which we have always found to be very helpful for having stability in her life.</p>\n<p>School for the morning until 1pm, afternoons to play, 5pm dinner, 6-8pm bedtime. Saturdays are free, Sundays have church and a play date, and then afternoons free. 1-2 hours of screen time a week.</p>\n<p>Over the past couple of weeks (basically since school started in January), she's expressed a feeling of being out of control of her life. She said things like 'I have no choice!' or 'when can I do what I want to do?!'. I point out to her she has <em>hours</em> to play in the afternoons.</p>\n<p>She has obligations, like cleaning up her toys/activities after herself, cleaning her plate after dinner, helping with the laundry, etc. There is a lot of grumbling and moaning and stamping of feet for these tasks.</p>\n<p>I can see she feels stifled and stuck in her own life. And while we all need to get used to having obligations and doing things we don't feel like doing, I am also at a loss for how to help her feel like she has more control over her life. How can I offer her control and independence, that she very clearly needs, while also keeping to our (unfortunately quite fixed and necessary) schedule?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","behavior","safety"],"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":846,"answer_count":1,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1707854390,"creation_date":1707403021,"question_id":43957,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43957/2-year-old-keeps-pulling-up-the-couch-cushions-and-laying-underneath-them-is-t","title":"2 year old keeps pulling up the couch cushions and laying underneath them - is this a problem?","body":"<p>My 2 year old has a new favorite habit - lifting up the couch cushions and putting himself underneath them.</p>\n<p>My wife and I are pretty cautious, and it's not very subtle that he's under there, so he's not in danger of being sat on - but he could be trying to eat food out from the nook of the couch.</p>\n<p>Is this a dangerous thing for him to be doing? And, how can we prevent him from doing it?  Pulling him out from it repeatedly doesn't seem to deter him, and encouraging him to sit on the couch or go somewhere else hasn't helped much either.</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","education","school"],"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":375,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1707696394,"creation_date":1707696394,"question_id":43962,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43962/why-wont-schools-allow-students-to-repeat-a-year","title":"Why won&#39;t schools allow students to repeat a year?","body":"<p>In a little over a year time our child will have to pick a secondary education (in the Netherlands).\nThrought friends with older children I have heard stories of children who were refused to repeat a year when they performed poorly. Insted they would either have to leave school or do the next year at a lower level.</p>\n<p>I should explain here that in the Netherlands a child can go through secondary education on different levels, this will in turn decide if they can go to University after school or Higher Vocational Education, or some other kind of Vocational Education.</p>\n<p>Back in my time the children who repeated a year invariably did so because they had spend the year smoking weed in the park and not doing their homework.\nAs a parent I don't see why in such cases a child should not repeat a year. By moving to a lower level the child will miss out even if they later return/continue education at a higher level. This is because they will have missed several years of study at a higher level. For math especially this is a problem since they will have missed some important topics.</p>\n<p>Is this a policy and if so what is the rationale? And is this just in the Netherelands or does it happen in other countries?</p>\n"},{"tags":["play-time","play","physical-activity"],"owner":{"account_id":457359,"reputation":745,"user_id":4718,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cc7c48c1181fdb3b78c2caf527a1812d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"I Like to Code","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4718/i-like-to-code"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":7775,"answer_count":10,"score":31,"last_activity_date":1708197075,"creation_date":1707984000,"question_id":43973,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43973/how-should-i-reconcile-the-concept-of-no-means-no-when-i-tease-my-5-year-old-d","title":"How should I reconcile the concept of &quot;no means no&quot; when I tease my 5-year-old during tickle play?","body":"<p>I have a 5-year-old girl.\nSometimes, she enjoys tickle play;\nfor instance, she will purposely say something a bit naughty/silly,\nwhile I am teaching her the piano or reading to her,\nand I will tickle her as a &quot;punishment&quot;.</p>\n<p>I discovered that a core part of making tickle play more fun is teasing.\nIn other words, I will pretend to not tickle her,\nand then I will suddenly tickle her a lot.\nIn my (perhaps uneducated) opinion,\nthere's a corresponding type of invitation on her part,\nwhere sometimes she will says &quot;Stop&quot;,\nbut she may actually mean &quot;Continue&quot;.\nThere have been times when she is not in the mood for tickling,\nor the tickles are too much;\nin such situations she gets angry or sends other nonverbal signals\nwhich give me the clear message that it's not the right time for tickles.</p>\n<p>Recently, my wife was furious at me when she witnessed us engaged in tickle play.\nI was saying something like, &quot;I am not tickling you&quot;,\nand then suddenly start tickling her briefly.\nMy wife was angry because she feels that\nit is critical for our daughter to learn &quot;No means No!&quot;,\nand also for her older siblings to understand this concept.\nShe brought up the point of the risk that\nour kids may encounter sexual predators who might gaslight them\nby saying things such as, &quot;You enjoy being touched.&quot;</p>\n<p>I feel quite confused because I think there is some truth in what my wife says.\nHowever, I can't imagine that tickle play makes sense\nif I have to ask my kid at every moment,\n&quot;Do you want a tickle now?&quot;\nThat would seem too structured.\nI feel like there is some value in me learning to read her cues,\nand her learning how to communicate when &quot;Stop&quot; means &quot;Stop&quot;,\nand when &quot;Stop&quot; means &quot;Continue&quot;.</p>\n<p><strong>How should I reconcile the concept of &quot;no means no&quot; / &quot;Stop means Stop&quot;\nwhen I tease my 5-year-old during tickle play?</strong></p>\n"},{"tags":["potty-training","tantrums"],"owner":{"account_id":30550684,"reputation":51,"user_id":45587,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKUL4iKMYWncjDKYU2RHUGgLEBIc7WZIMiFA9YFsrr4QQ=k-s256","display_name":"Olg","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45587/olg"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":362,"answer_count":1,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1730811599,"creation_date":1708019398,"last_edit_date":1730811599,"question_id":43976,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43976/4-year-old-potty-regression-but-concerned-about-the-hysteria-more-than-the-regre","title":"4 year old potty regression but concerned about the hysteria more than the regression","body":"<p>My 4 year old daughter is perfectly potty trained (with no accidents) since last June. She wears a diaper at night. Lately she wants to wear a diaper all day because her best friend has regressed. She is also a 'deeply feeling' child and is extraordinarily attached to this notion of babyhood. I've tried explaining to her that wearing underwear doesn't mean she is not a baby anymore etc. But either way she is a sensitive one and everyone can see that. However, I've started/attempted to maintain boundaries - no diapers or pull ups during daytime and only at night. It is not working - she won't go out without them, she will cry hysterically. Now when she is hysterical, she starts to scratch herself (no marks) or she tries stuff that shift my attention from the diaper issue at hand to her mental/physical health. Then I have to relent. Should we seek a counselor? Because whatever this is - rage, anxiety, or manipulation - is a bit scary!? I fear that our confrontations are making her more attached to diapers and less trusting of us. I've lost my temper in the past weeks, then cooled down and have tried to let her have her 'moment' while staying in the room, tried talking nicely but firmly. I fear I've not been consistent but I also feel that her extreme reaction (self-harming?) is problematic? Also she is still breastfed and is very attached to me, and I can see any rift between us causes her heartache. I am so confused! :(</p>\n<p>P.s: she doesn't have random triggers for tantrums - but she has 3 or 4 things that she is fixated on. Other non-negotiables in the past have been winterwears and playdates (she doesn't like them), she likes to dress herself, she likes to stay in character for days at an end. These areas can lead to meltdowns. Otherwise, she eats well, she is healthy, she is funny, imaginative,and has a fantastic memory.</p>\n"},{"tags":["adhd","programming"],"owner":{"account_id":24031229,"reputation":131,"user_id":45593,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e54b03789af6ce6eb55d53d16527f5bb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Craving_gold","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45593/craving-gold"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":450,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1710386455,"creation_date":1708104774,"last_edit_date":1708313862,"question_id":43984,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43984/how-can-i-teach-a-teenager-living-with-adhd-coding","title":"How can I teach a teenager living with adhd coding","body":"<p>I'm an African. I have a teenage relative living with ADHD. I think he's very brilliant. Can someone recommend some online platforms where he can learn how to code or some methods that can help him to code, please</p>\n"},{"tags":["speech"],"owner":{"account_id":897891,"reputation":141,"user_id":45594,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/239c4db22bae3eaec81e3838bb0161f6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"djnz0feh","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45594/djnz0feh"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":405,"answer_count":2,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1709822557,"creation_date":1708105048,"question_id":43985,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43985/recovering-from-selective-mutism","title":"Recovering from selective mutism","body":"<p>I have two twin daughters 12 year old, with selective mutism (SM) who used not to talk in school at all but have been steadily improving - talking to friends in their ear at the playground - and can talk to the teacher at close quarters when needed. Answering questions or reading out loud in class still seems some way off.</p>\n<p>What is it like to 'recover' from selective mutism? What can be done to encourage that letting go of that last bit of fear? I met one person who says she had SM up until 9 years old (also a twin) but she 'snapped out of it'. Is this a typical experience for SM sufferers or is it more gradual?</p>\n"},{"tags":["extended-family","family"],"owner":{"account_id":30516087,"reputation":21,"user_id":45574,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9bd7736cbebc28803008781d21a15c88?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Tom","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45574/tom"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":256,"closed_date":1708269937,"answer_count":0,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1708274960,"creation_date":1708154943,"last_edit_date":1708274960,"question_id":43989,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43989/what-to-do-as-a-cousin","closed_reason":"Needs details or clarity","title":"What to do as a cousin","body":"<p>So I have 2 female cousins who are 17 years old.  That I have not seen in 5 years until last July.  I use to take them to the park, I taught them how to swim, and I use to take them to trips and out to sports so that they could have a great life later on.  I left the country for 5 years and have come back to see them again 7 months ago.  Our family was always tight knit. I have come back now and the entire family is separated.  The dynamics of the family members has changed.  My cousins parents are always fighting.  The father is never in their life and the mother doesn't care about them.  They are both adopted and their true family members are from a life of drugs and prison.  Them being in their teenage years they are trying to grow but they are doing some extreme things.  They are both allowed to have their boyfriend over a couple times a week closed doors and the parents don't ask anything.  They have both expressed ideas of self harm to their mom.\nThey are on anti pregnancy medication.  They hqve both told me that they took various kinds of drugs in the past, nothing hard.  They have told me that they were abused physically in forms of punishment by their parents such as beating them.  Their parents are not in their lives at all.  The younger one sneaked out of her room last month to meet someone who picked her up in a car.  Which is insane to me.  They seem to be completely disconnected from the family and lost.  I have expressed to the mom that this is could be chaotic and dangerous and come to an abrupt end but she just doesn't care.  She feels like she is taking care of someone else's kids.  My relationship with them as a cousin is not the best right now.  They keep on pushing me away whenever i tell them abput the dangers of the world or whenever i try to compare the challenges that i went through in my life but they tell me to mind my own business its not my life.\nI am at a loss and day to day this is messing me up just thinking about it. They are both in their last year of high-school and I am scared for them and their lives.  What can I do?\nI want to finally be someone that can stabilize them on their road in their lives and have a fun connection with them till the end. Or at least move in that direction so that they can be on a healthy and positive but fun road in their life</p>\n"},{"tags":["adult-child"],"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user45612"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":4634,"accepted_answer_id":44001,"answer_count":3,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1751150616,"creation_date":1708256798,"question_id":43993,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43993/my-adult-daughter-is-a-heavy-smoker-because-of-me-is-there-anything-i-can-do","title":"My adult daughter is a heavy smoker because of me, is there anything I can do?","body":"<p>My daughter started smoking pretty early, stealing cigarettes from me. Throughout her teens I allowed her to smoke and even provided cigarettes to her. I thought that fighting her habit would be both kind of hypocritical of me (since I was also a smoker) as well as pointless (since she'd probably find a way to steal my cigarettes anyway).</p>\n<p>Now she's in her 20s. I managed to finally quit smoking a few years ago. She, however, is smoking more than ever. Even other people mention how much she smokes. She insists she loves it and will never quit. On top of that she also drinks more than I'd hope she would (though her drinking is nowhere near as severe as her smoking).</p>\n<p>At this point I suppose there is nothing more I can do. But I blame myself for the current situation. My past actions when she was a teen result in what I can see now. I gave my own daughter a life marred by addiction and, eventually, disease, on top of multiple other difficulties since smoking is less and less tolerated nowadays (though, at least for now, she somehow avoids most of this since she works from home - but for how long?)</p>\n<p>I almost lost it when I accidentally saw the photo of her she gave to her boyfriend. It depicts her sitting in a bar behind a thick cloud of smoke, with a cigarette in one hand and a big mug of beer in another hand. Needless to say her boyfriend is into this kind of lifestyle as well.</p>\n<p>Is it really the case that as of now there is nothing left for me to do but to accept the consequences of my past actions and live in guilt? Or is there any way I can somehow, anyhow, help my daughter?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","sleep","safety"],"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":5617,"answer_count":5,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1709979912,"creation_date":1708393801,"question_id":43995,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/43995/toddler-keeps-lifting-mattress-up-and-putting-his-legs-into-the-mattress-rack","title":"Toddler keeps lifting mattress up and putting his legs into the mattress rack - how to prevent?","body":"<p>We have a large crib that converted into our toddler's bed.  It has served us well for some time, but recently there's been a problem concerning our toddler's night-time or nap-time habits.</p>\n<p>For some reason, our toddler greatly enjoys flipping the mattress up.  Sometimes onto its edge, other times entirely off the bed and onto the floor.</p>\n<p>That alone is not a problem - the problem is that once the mattress is out, his natural curiosity has him stepping into the area the mattress once was - the mattress rack.  And once in there, he has a great deal of difficulty getting his legs <em>out</em> of the mattress rack.</p>\n<p>What can we do to prevent our toddler from getting into that area?  Or, what can we do to prevent him from getting his legs caught between the bars of the mattress rack?</p>\n"},{"tags":["primary-schooler"],"owner":{"account_id":30628631,"reputation":169,"user_id":45643,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocL-hgykOWH_MePW9qeCbXrCoYqScd23XrGS9QhXnp4Y=k-s256","display_name":"Dima ","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45643/dima"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":12829,"protected_date":1708980921,"answer_count":7,"score":16,"last_activity_date":1709133509,"creation_date":1708874341,"question_id":44006,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44006/my-nephew-is-10-years-old-he-does-not-read-the-exam-questions-properly-which-le","title":"My nephew is 10 years old, he does not read the exam questions properly which leads to many mistakes although he knows the answer, what can I do?","body":"<p>My nephew is 10 years old, he does not read the exam questions properly which leads to many mistakes although he knows the answer, what can I do ?</p>\n"},{"tags":["infant","daycare","attention"],"owner":{"account_id":2966345,"reputation":1797,"user_id":31475,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Wmix3.png?s=256","display_name":"Martijn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31475/martijn"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":602,"accepted_answer_id":44038,"answer_count":2,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1727355081,"creation_date":1709063520,"last_edit_date":1727191368,"question_id":44019,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44019/my-10mnd-old-wants-100-of-the-time-attention-how-can-i-reduce-this","title":"My 10mnd old wants 100% of the time attention, how can I reduce this?","body":"<p>My 10mnd old demands 100% of the time attention from others other than his parents, how can I reduce this?</p>\n<p>When I or his mom are around, he can play somewhat solo. I give him attention for a significant amount of time, but also want to practice playing solo. I dont expect 100% independency, he can entertain himself about an hour or so, but we do need to be close by.<br />\nHis mother and both his grandmothers experience the same thing.</p>\n<p>However, when we bring him to the daycare and pick him up at the end of a day, we get the feedback that he has been practising for <em>tornado siren</em> all day (he has a good set of lungs), unless they give full attention, which they cant because there are more kids.</p>\n<p>This is coming to the point where the daycare is dropping hints that they might not be able to do this for a lot longer. Their main argument is that this can't be fun for him and while its not really a problem now, this might soon turn in an a bad experience permanently. The guardian there has been doing this for about 30 years and she says he is one of the most difficult cases she's ever had.</p>\n<p>He is like that with more people. My father (who's better with kids 2yrs+) has difficulty with him too. My kid can be (and is often) described as pretty intense. It is very obvious wether or not he is liking something, he is happy with the same intensity.</p>\n<p>I'm looking for something we can do. My first though was to &quot;leave&quot; (e.g. do laundry a small distance over) him for 5mins, then come back. Then increase that more and more.<br />\nWhen he is at the daycare, he will not stop crying at all. Practicing this has booked us no results at all.<br />\nWe've asked all people babysitting him to practice this and they all do, and it works for them, as long as there are no others to share attention with.</p>\n<p>When the daycare guardian picks him up and gives the attention, he stops crying immediatly, I'm confident he sees her as a safe person too. The guardian suggests that he might be overstimulated because when the other kids go to bed, he becomes easier. But when I ask if she then gives him more private attention, the answer is yes. Me and his mother also are not really under the impression that overstimulation is the issue, as we bring him to more situations where there is plenty of stimuli, and he doesnt really seem to impressed by it. He also like a bit more wild playing, such as swings, or placing him on one of the kid cars and drive him around.</p>\n<p>His week is like so:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Monday: His mother has him (private attention, but then he plays well enough)</li>\n<li>Tuesday: Daycare, significant crying when left alone (even for a few seconds!)</li>\n<li>Wednesday: His mother has him (private attention, but then he plays well enough)</li>\n<li>Tuesday: His grandmother has him (private attention, but then he plays well enough)</li>\n<li>Friday: I have him (private attention, but then he plays well enough)</li>\n<li>Weekend: I and his mother have him (private attention, but then he plays well enough)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We're out of ideas how to approach this and we need the daycare to work. Does anyone know a good technique we can try?</p>\n<p>My mother described me the same when I was young, easy but continues, she left me to cry and that seemed to work for me, but when my kid is with us, no real issues. And letting him cry does nothing for the daycare.</p>\n<p>He has no issues with sleeping, wherever he is. At the daycare he becomes so upset he barely eats/drinks, worsening everything.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Little addendum: The daycare is very much in line with our philosophy. She takes them outside often, lets them play in the rain (she has raincoats) and stomp in puddles (she has boots). She promotes creative behaviour (like couloring), they sing together (I dont like singing, but its a fun kid activity). She is not a neatfreak, not scared of a booger etc, but does care about hygiene to a realistic standard. She is of the &quot;emphisise positive, redirect negative&quot; style (unless something serieusly wrong), creating self esteem. We do that too.</p>\n"},{"tags":["fears"],"owner":{"account_id":2862218,"reputation":1110,"user_id":13340,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":25,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ae7fd2628c87be64ce65d6dd6294c9c1?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"NeutronStar","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13340/neutronstar"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":13783,"protected_date":1709806528,"answer_count":8,"score":13,"last_activity_date":1710273790,"creation_date":1709497346,"last_edit_date":1710273790,"question_id":44025,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44025/6-year-old-wont-go-to-the-bathroom-by-herself-anymore-because-of-being-scared","title":"6 year old won&#39;t go to the bathroom by herself anymore, because of being scared. What to do?","body":"<p>My 6 y.o. is what Dr. Becky (if you know her) would call a &quot;deeply feeling child.&quot; Every day is either the best or worst day ever, and she usually declares each day as one or the other multiple times per day. Everything is an emergency when it comes up, she wants our full attention all of the time even when we can't give it, talks a lot, and screams and yells a lot. Just to paint a picture.</p>\n<p>Yesterday, she started asking about if anything lives in the toilet and can come out while she is using it. We told her there is not. She told us about a noise that came from the bathroom when she turned on the faucet or something, which makes sense because I had had the water to the house off earlier that day for some repair work and some pretty intense gurgling afterwards is normal. Despite our explanations of why that sound occurred, she is scared of bugs or animals coming out of the toilet while she is peeing.</p>\n<p>Right now, she is complaining about being scared of going to the bathroom, and has been crying about it for 10 minutes. We've explained to her that everything is fine and there's nothing to be afraid of. Last night, I helped her go to the bathroom when she was scared, thinking that would be the end of that, but it apparently is not. We've decided to not continue going with her to the bathroom, so that this doesn't become a regular thing, and she absolutely refuses to go.</p>\n<p>She was also one difficult to potty train. It took probably about two or two and a half years (from the time she was two and a half) for her to stop wetting herself regularly, and even now she goes through phases where she regularly pees in her underwear a little bit before going to the bathroom.</p>\n<p>How can we convince her to go to the bathroom by herself? All of the other things we help her with, that we walk on eggshells around her to try to help her, I cannot add going to the bathroom regularly with her to that list.</p>\n<p><strong>Edit:</strong> After two days, she stopped showing any fear of the bathroom.</p>\n"},{"tags":["family"],"owner":{"account_id":30691166,"reputation":11,"user_id":45682,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocKujswzaLPVxRCCgTaEqGWwTHwnnWu2q3C-V7CHOPgj=k-s256","display_name":"TBI LP","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45682/tbi-lp"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":497,"closed_date":1709892659,"answer_count":1,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1709643213,"creation_date":1709581401,"question_id":44034,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44034/father-in-law-possible-pedophile","closed_reason":"Not suitable for this site","title":"Father in law possible pedophile","body":"<p>I’m dealing with a very tricky situation. I innocently came across my father in law liking photos of minor children on Facebook. My heart was pounding like there must be some sort of mistake here. Nope. I dug deep and found over 200+ photos of him liking. All minor children in swimsuits, regular clothing but short shorts, see through shirts and even shorts showing body parts. My husband now knows and we are so deeply disturbed. We do not know how to bring this up in a way that he doesn’t do something rash. I don’t think he would but you never know. We have two young boys and this is not ok for them to be around someone like this. We do not have a good relationship with his parents just very distant but this needs to be spoken about. Even if we have boys this is still not ok. We are in a tough position and a very awkward one.</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","motivation","homework"],"owner":{"account_id":14366070,"reputation":151,"user_id":45711,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-xI22mA0NLM8/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAA/APUIFaPkda97At2-1d9TnlSm4EnxPyLNXQ/mo/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"My Full Name","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45711/my-full-name"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":938,"answer_count":2,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1710547370,"creation_date":1709845636,"question_id":44046,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44046/how-to-make-11-years-old-kid-do-homework","title":"How to make 11 years old kid do homework","body":"<p>I have an 11 years old daughter who goes to a fairly &quot;laid back&quot; school - in particular they didn't use to have homework regularly. Occasionally she brought back some exercises that she did immediately after coming home - but this was maybe once every two months. However, recently she got a new teacher who insist on giving homework every week, for the weekend - and I have really hard time to make her do her homework. Apparently all of her classmates do the homework in the school in the afternoon, but she prefers to play instead.</p>\n<p>She complains that it's unfair that her younger siblings don't have homework. I tried to motivate her positively (<em>&quot;If you do your homework, you get cookies&quot;</em>), but to no avail. I tried to motivate her negatively (&quot;<em>If you don't finish your homework, you can't watch TV.</em>&quot;, &quot;<em>If you don't do your homework, don't get good grades, won't succeed in life.</em>&quot;), but it just lead to &quot;<em>Right, I'm going to stab myself with a knife, because I don't want to live if I have to do homework. You don't love me anyway because you want me to do homework.</em>&quot;. Although I think she's overdramatising (there was a kind of &quot;psychological checkup&quot; at school and the psychiatrist said she doesn't suffer from depression), there was suicide in the family, so her choice of words does scare me. Also sometimes she does her homework without me asking here - my problem is that when she doesn't do it, I can't motivate her.</p>\n<p>The problem is unfortunately a little more generic: I also have problem motivating her to go outside, go to an excursion, walk the dog, check out a kids-specific exhibition at a museum, etc. Sometimes she gets into this state of mind (&quot;<em>I hate the world, especially you!</em>&quot;) where I simply can't reach her and she's not willing to do anything.</p>\n<p>I'm a little loss at here. My wife says it's &quot;normal teen stuff&quot;, but due to her work schedule she's often not at home at the same time as the kids, so can't help me. I've googled and found a few ideas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Set aside a specific time to do her homework. She'd just go to her own room and do nothing or read a book.</li>\n<li>Let her do the homework while I'm doing some chores, so she sees she's not the only one who works. She'd just go to her own room and do nothing or read a book.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I don't remember from my childhood that I had this much trouble with homework - I used get over it quickly, so I could play. Do you have any better ideas?</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","primary-schooler","activities"],"owner":{"account_id":1665264,"reputation":2243,"user_id":7778,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":33,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1edb320793125bf1d36ed27b87b9c51?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"localhost","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7778/localhost"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":4691,"answer_count":10,"score":13,"last_activity_date":1736189592,"creation_date":1709990677,"question_id":44050,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44050/activities-besides-tv","title":"Activities besides tv","body":"<p>My 8yo, after school runs a tv marathon (as did I when I was kid) but that is because there is nothing to do for him, I got him books but he study/read in one go and finishes it. I live in UK so weather is always gloomy because of rain, so they cannot play outside.</p>\n<p>I do not like the habit of watching tv unless we go outside to shop, or he find something worth doing, got him a drone (without camera) he mastered it in 1 month and since haven't touched it claming it is boring without a camera (which is out of my budget right now).</p>\n<p>I got him into coding, with kids websites, he likes it but rather go on other profiles and see what they made, like it (as seen in his acitivity), I want him to learn skills and get off the tv and only use tv for a little bit.</p>\n<p>My small one 4 yo is on his footstep but I know if I can change my elder behaviour the little one follows.</p>\n<p>What should I do to get him to do anything. I really do not know what they can do.</p>\n"},{"tags":["middle-childhood","social-development","sports","competitiveness"],"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":524,"answer_count":3,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1712718771,"creation_date":1710164697,"question_id":44057,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44057/help-child-dealing-with-soccer-jock","title":"Help child dealing with soccer jock","body":"<p>My 10-year old started playing soccer about 6 months ago as the new kid in an existing team. His team gets along well for the most part and most the children know each other from school.\nAs parents we all get along too.</p>\n<p>During matches and sometimes even during training tempers will flare. Mostly this consists of mouthing off against a member the other team for pushing or standing too close to the player that is kicking the ball back in the field (I don't know how this soccerrule translates in English.)\nBut generally they show good sportmanship.</p>\n<p>There is one kid who is very mouthy very often. To the other team, to the referee and to his own teammates.\nHis father is the team coach and he has to repeat the phrase 'Quiet now, Bobby!' very often. ('Bobby' is not his real name)\nThe kid in question is among the better players, but certainly not the best. He playes in a forward position so he often scores, adding to his ego.</p>\n<p>My child has a very hard time dealing with this boy. When my kid makes a mistake or when the other kid is annoyed by something, he just yells at my kid in passing that he is terribly bad at soccer.\nWhich he is not, but he is among the not so good players in the team, he has way less experience and he is very selfconscious.\nThe annoying kid will also misbehave against the really good players, but then everyone knows he is full of shit and the target will easily push back verbally and phsically. Yes there is pushing, from what I saw was mostly in jest but it looked as if it could escalate.</p>\n<p>We talked about this recently with another teammember and his dad, and aparently the boy misbehaved more in previous years and was send off the field several times for it.\nIt seems to me the coach (who is also the father of the annoying kid) is trying very hard to be a fair and levelled for the whole team.\nI want to help my child deal with the situation and I want to avoid doing something stupid that will make it worse for him.</p>\n<p>Up to now I have not done anything but comfort my child.</p>\n<p>I think there are many options for acting but I have a hard time deciding how to proceed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>comfort my child</li>\n<li>give my child directions on how to handle this (but how should he handle it?)</li>\n<li>talk to the coach</li>\n<li>talk to the team</li>\n<li>be the fitful mom and tell the kid off there and then, see what happens</li>\n<li>....</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"tags":["primary-schooler","school"],"owner":{"account_id":30804762,"reputation":179,"user_id":45759,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c6786eb485a752de82488ad18fab4c5f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Simone","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45759/simone"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":8792,"answer_count":5,"score":17,"last_activity_date":1712846030,"creation_date":1710554634,"last_edit_date":1710624670,"question_id":44069,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44069/teacher-telling-4th-grade-student-i-hate-you","title":"Teacher telling 4th grade student &quot;I hate you&quot;","body":"<p>Long story short my 9 year old informed me that her teacher told her, &quot;I hate you.&quot; The principal conducted an investigation and 1 child said they heard the teacher say something to my child about hate. The principal then asked the teacher and he said the teacher stated he couldn't remember what he said. He told the teacher to apologize and I demanded my child be removed from the class. The principal with some hesitation removed her from the class. I filled out a DASA report and they deemed it unfounded. Which is ridiculous. Do I go further with this? I don't want the school to retaliate against my child however I don't want them to sweep this under the rug. Please help what would you do?</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","brushing-teeth"],"owner":{"account_id":22227260,"reputation":557,"user_id":45224,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJwzcgOLTOqjclf1YW1S8sz5SxiWAIY191kUd2-r=k-s256","display_name":"multitaskingmom","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45224/multitaskingmom"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":227,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1712052922,"creation_date":1710789415,"question_id":44080,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44080/teeth-brushing-for-preschoolers","title":"Teeth Brushing for Preschoolers","body":"<p>I have a technical question about tooth brushing.</p>\n<p>I'm finding it really difficult to brush the cheek-facing &quot;front&quot; side of the tooth.</p>\n<p>I myself can fit the whole toothbrush head between my inner cheek and teeth when brushing that side, but if I try that method with my kids, they say that it hurts.</p>\n<p>It might be some combination of having smaller mouths and not knowing how to relax their faces for that part.</p>\n<p>I'm already using kid-sized toothbrushes with very small heads.</p>\n<p>Has anyone else had this problem before?</p>\n<p>Thank you!</p>\n"},{"tags":["potty-training"],"owner":{"account_id":30855126,"reputation":29,"user_id":45787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6f5c6a609a2360b8552599c4c77ff3d4?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Pwoods ","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45787/pwoods"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":617,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1712715634,"creation_date":1710925574,"last_edit_date":1711279218,"question_id":44083,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44083/4-year-old-son-peeing-on-himself","title":"4 year old son peeing on himself","body":"<p>My 4 year old son is very very smart, but he pees on himself at home.</p>\n<p>Sometimes when we go out he goes to the bathroom in public. Some days he wets the bed at night at home, other days he gets up middle of the night and go potty. During the day time sometimes he goes to the bathroom busy or not and other times he doesn’t.</p>\n<p>He does go to daycare and doesn’t pee on himself there. Also he goes to his grandma house just about every weekend if not every other weekend and sometimes for a week at a time and never pees over there. I can’t understand what he’s doing over there that he just can’t seem to do at home to not pee on himself. I’ve tried asking him why does he pee on himself all he say is &quot;I don't know&quot;.</p>\n<p>I tried timeout, tried taking everything away, no tv no toys and playing. I did recently have a 1 year old so that definitely could be why he’s doing this because it has been about 2 years give or take that he has been doing this so he’s not that little baby no more. I also have a 10 year old.</p>\n<p>I’ve tried everything. I took him to the doctor to see a specialist and they said everything is normal inside wise. I just can’t understand why sometimes he goes and most times he don’t and don’t care if he wet himself or not I don’t know what else to do I’ve tried so many different things any suggestions plz.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","bedtime"],"owner":{"account_id":30919454,"reputation":7,"user_id":45796,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fecabcb2eaea030920196529fb3c4340?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Jg13","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45796/jg13"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":302,"answer_count":2,"score":-1,"last_activity_date":1729527469,"creation_date":1711115086,"question_id":44088,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44088/i-work-late-and-my-4yr-old-wont-go-to-sleep","title":"I work late and my 4yr old wont go to sleep","body":"<p>I work late and usually don't get home till 2 or 3 a.m. and my 4yr old is wide awake. Gives trouble to go to bed early when I'm away working. I don't want my kid going to sleep at 2 or 3 in the morning.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","safety"],"owner":{"account_id":30975235,"reputation":11,"user_id":45805,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocLHMNvL8z_LN4nuokX_lz0qpCY_39jY-hKQf1DtLs8z=k-s256","display_name":"KT88","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45805/kt88"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":176,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1711644998,"creation_date":1711371844,"last_edit_date":1711644998,"question_id":44091,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44091/my-18-month-old-keeps-pulling-the-couch-cushions-off-the-pullout-sofa-how-to-k","title":"My 18 month old keeps pulling the couch cushions off the pullout sofa - how to keep him safe?","body":"<p>We have a couch that has a pull out bed for guests. My son's newest habit (18 months old) is pulling up the cushions and running across where the pull out bed is. We just bought this couch (too expensive to replace), and I am scared to death he is going to break an ankle or get a big gash from running across the exposed but put away pull out bed and falling into the parts where the metal that keeps the pullout bed folded into the couch is. Any suggestions to help me make this safer for him?</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","bilingual","language"],"owner":{"account_id":31015011,"reputation":61,"user_id":45814,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cfc19308b5f7efe177df36ffb851abc0?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Ladymama","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45814/ladymama"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1049,"answer_count":4,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1711955213,"creation_date":1711507721,"question_id":44092,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44092/i-messed-up-and-didnt-teach-my-4-year-old-much-english-how-do-i-start-now","title":"I messed up and didn&#39;t teach my 4 year old much English, how do I start now?","body":"<p>Any ideas how I can teach my 4 yr old son English, even though I'm sure I've missed some vital window by not doing 1 parent 1 language?</p>\n<p>A little background: English is one of my native languages but my husband only speaks our local language. When my son was a baby, it felt exhausting for me to speak separate languages and too complicated for my husband not to know what I was saying all day. So I ended up speaking to my son only in our local language with a little English thrown in through songs and games.</p>\n<p>Now I've realized it would be really nice for him to be able to speak English, especially since I'm considering one day moving to an English speaking place again and a part of our extended family only speaks English. Since I didn't do one parent one language, what's the next option?</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","behavior","primary-schooler","depression"],"owner":{"account_id":1665264,"reputation":2243,"user_id":7778,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":33,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1edb320793125bf1d36ed27b87b9c51?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"localhost","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7778/localhost"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":206,"answer_count":1,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1712221187,"creation_date":1712137427,"question_id":44100,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44100/how-to-make-sure-kids-prioritize-keeping-in-contact-when-away","title":"How to make sure kids prioritize keeping in contact when away","body":"<p>My wife and kids has gone abroad,although were suppose to go together but I couldn't. so it is my kids first time without me, although they went to a pilgrimge site but kids mostly stay at home with other family member. What suprise me most is my kids haven't called me on their on, e.g. My wife when encountered that she doesn't call or make me talk to kids, she is always telling how busy she is etc etc which I get but My wife has a habit when she is with her side of family, she is least bothered to call or reply me citing again she was too busy, I say this because when we went to our home country, she wanted to stay with her side of family and that happened again, yet when any kids don't feel well, she cause a havoc and blame me for not keeping updated.</p>\n<p>What I wanted to ask, why my kids are not feeling the urge of missing dad and calling me? I say it because mostly my wife mobile is with kids, even in morning when she is sleeping and kids want it to play games, they know my profile in whatsApp and do access it (so it is not like that my kids do not know).</p>\n<p>This is a recurring thing with my wife and I know for sure now, she always want to sound busy when it is priority, and I do not want my kids to follow that way in her footsteps, because they are. My mother always said about my wife how she doesn't call but now I am certain why and this is something I do not want my kids to have.</p>\n<p>N.B - I do text asking about kids and health, however I feel ignored because of un replied message and call, but calling only when she has free herself or lying in bed. I do not want my kids to treat me as a last priority but how can I put that in them because tomorrow my kids will grow up seeing the mom doesn't prioritize father (but only say, not mean it) and will follow same ?</p>\n"},{"tags":["newborn","development","feeding"],"owner":{"account_id":1891677,"reputation":151,"user_id":33732,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/cP8pZ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Mark Amery","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33732/mark-amery"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1793,"answer_count":1,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1712560179,"creation_date":1712515124,"question_id":44103,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44103/why-are-recommended-newborn-feed-volumes-much-bigger-than-typical-stomach-size-f","title":"Why are recommended newborn feed volumes much bigger than typical stomach size for the same age?","body":"<p>Many sources will tell you the following supposed facts about a newborn baby's biology, or very similar ones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A newborn's stomach is tiny and it remains quite small for a while. For instance, one source says that by day 10, <a href=\"https://www.healthline.com/health/baby/your-newborns-stomach-size-is-smaller-than-you-think\" rel=\"noreferrer\">her stomach is only the size of a golf ball</a>. This is about <strong>40ml</strong>.</li>\n<li>A baby's stomach <a href=\"https://blog.babycues.com/2017/may/stomach-capacity-of-newborns/\" rel=\"noreferrer\">cannot stretch</a>.</li>\n<li>It takes <a href=\"https://www.chop.edu/pages/breastfeeding-tips-beginners\" rel=\"noreferrer\">1-2 hours for consumed breast milk to leave the stomach</a>, while <a href=\"https://healthy.kaiserpermanente.org/health-wellness/health-encyclopedia/he.bottle-feeding-disadvantages-for-babies.tj8029\" rel=\"noreferrer\">formula takes twice as long to digest</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(I've included arbitrary links to back up each claim by way of example, but I see claims along these lines everywhere, albeit often with somewhat different numbers.)</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, <a href=\"https://www.costco.co.uk/medias/sys_master/images/h86/h17/97084000239646.webp\" rel=\"noreferrer\">infant formula packaging advises giving a baby of age 0-2 weeks <strong>90ml</strong> of formula per feed</a>, and a review of the notes I've kept on my week-old daughter's feeds reveals we gave her a 70ml formula feed on day 6 and an 80ml pumped breast milk feed today, on day 7.</p>\n<p>The apparent contradiction is hopefully obvious; the amounts of liquid we are recommended to feed newborns - and that they can observably consume! - are at some ages <em>more than twice as large</em> as the supposed size of their supposedly inelastic stomachs that the fluid will supposedly take over an hour to pass through. This doesn't seem to just be a matter of babies developing at different speeds, either; I'm not (as far as I can tell) comparing a maximum feed volume against a minimum stomach size or anything like that. Instead the <em>typical</em> amounts we're told a newborn should consume are simply much larger than what we're told a newborn's <em>typical</em> stomach size is.</p>\n<p>My baby thankfully hasn't exploded, but if everything I've been told about her biology is true, I'm not really sure how that can be the case. Is there something I'm missing about how her body works that resolves the paradox - for instance, after a feed of more milk than her stomach size, does milk/formula somehow wait in the oesophagus until there's space in the stomach? (This isn't how I understand the oesophagus to work.) Or, when she's overfed, does milk get forced out of the end of the stomach undigested? (Then what happens to it next? And is this problematic or at least wasteful?) Or is the contradiction real, and the explanation simply that the claims people make about stomach size aren't true?</p>\n"},{"tags":["breastfeeding","night-time"],"owner":{"account_id":11363645,"reputation":261,"user_id":45626,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e9bf10e5b98d3834ba27b89e60cafe7b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"BenjyTec","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45626/benjytec"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":496,"answer_count":2,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1715852369,"creation_date":1712656345,"last_edit_date":1712736070,"question_id":44105,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44105/how-often-should-i-breastfeed-my-baby-at-night","title":"How often should I breastfeed my baby at night?","body":"<p>My daughter (10 months old) is recently waking up several times per night, wanting to be breastfed. This happens every 2 hours, so 4-5 times per night. She wakes up and requests to be breastfed, and if I don't, she will start crying. She does not accept a pacifier instead.</p>\n<p>We already started feeding there, and progressed to a point where she barely is breastfed over the day, most time eating semisolids.</p>\n<p>I started wondering whether it can actually be that she gets hungry so quickly/often? Or could it be that she actually is aiming for something else?</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","diet"],"owner":{"account_id":22144838,"reputation":820,"user_id":44482,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d683b493fe09b71780dcd9b37f227315?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"rando","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/44482/rando"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":561,"closed_date":1712700773,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1712681924,"creation_date":1712669818,"last_edit_date":1712680081,"question_id":44107,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44107/our-teenage-son-doesnt-like-eating-because-he-doesnt-want-to-be-like-us","closed_reason":"Not suitable for this site","title":"Our teenage son doesn&#39;t like eating because he &quot;doesn&#39;t want to be like us&quot;","body":"<p>(of note, both of us parents are prediabetic)</p>\n<p>I am a parent of my thirteen year old son, and he has started to refrain from eating food in general. At first, it was subtle, but now he outright refuses or offers great resistance to eating food because he &quot;doesn't want to be like us when [he] grows up&quot;. While we are prediabetic and want to prevent anything like that from happening to him in the future, we also want to encourage him to eat a healthy amount of food for his age, especially since he is growing and he is in an athletics program. He seems to get most annoyed when he catches us watching health videos, and while we try to hide them from him, he still expresses dissent. How can we encourage our son to eat a healthy amount of food?</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-teen","social-development"],"owner":{"account_id":8110015,"reputation":129,"user_id":38350,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c46678bb4b3e585accf8e1a0cd4c0810?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"e-Fungus","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38350/e-fungus"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":5226,"accepted_answer_id":44285,"answer_count":2,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1722953181,"creation_date":1712768484,"last_edit_date":1712937659,"question_id":44114,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44114/good-tv-shows-for-teen-that-promote-good-values","title":"Good TV shows for teen that promote good values","body":"<p>My teen's teachers are complaining that he talks and act a bit too much like the current celebrity youtubers.</p>\n<p>Are there still any good TV shows suitable for teenagers, with reasonable social interactions between teens, that will still captivate this generation that don't want TV anymore?</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-teen","self-control"],"owner":{"account_id":3690277,"reputation":741,"user_id":39807,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/WREQP.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Ben","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39807/ben"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":251,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1712805552,"creation_date":1712805552,"question_id":44115,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44115/dealing-with-10-12-yer-old-impulsiveness","title":"Dealing with 10-12 yer old impulsiveness","body":"<p>I have 2 sons, aged 10 and 12 and lately they have been dealing with a lot of different things.</p>\n<p>So far, their uncle died rather suddenly after Christmas, then my dog died (old age), and now the widowed aunt is living with us, during her grieving period.</p>\n<p>Amongst all of this, the boys have had issues with impulsiveness. The 10 year old has always had issues with stealing, which has been reduced to lollies, rather than some of the bigger things he used to steal, but is now not going to sleep - we have to force him to stay in bed, play music, turn the lights off, and monitor that he is actually staying in bed.</p>\n<p>The 12 year old has recieved a laptop to be used for school, and has managed to steal the WiFi password, (which I am yet to change, though it was already a custom password - he had retrieved it from his iPad) and claims that it simply &quot;automatically connects&quot; whenever he gets caught using it when he shouldn't. We do allow him to use it for an hour or so after school for homework/assessments, but disconnect him afterwards. (I work in IT so I make sure that he shouldn't be able to access it when he is not allowed).</p>\n<p>They have however both grown more impulsive - waking up in the middle of the night to connect to the WiFi, or to play video games in the loungeroom.</p>\n<p>They both have an iPad, which is again limited to timeframes of when they can it and how long for. They have also connected this behaviour to the recent events, but they refuse to come to us to talk about it, instead calling &quot;kids helpline&quot; in Australia, to express their oppressed situation. But when we approach them, they state that they don't feel comfortable expressing themselves to us.</p>\n<p>For reference, we allow them to use their iPads any time after lunch, until bedtime, but restrict certain things such as YouTube, but allow free access to puzzle games (machinarium) or creative apps (duolingo or &quot;blek&quot;) or simple games like relaxing fishing games. During school days this is limited to the afternoons only, before the evening processes begin - afternoon chores, dinner, bedtime process.</p>\n<p>Video games are limited to an hour (minecraft, mariokart, Super Smash bros), but we do allow extensions on these based on behaviour.</p>\n<p>We express an open household, helping each other with chores, rewards for good behaviour (more free time on devices, or treats, etc), consequencesfor bad behaviour, such as temporarily removing access to the focus of the &quot;crime&quot;, like devices for the day, etc. Cooperation, consideration of each other, and communication, different methods to do so, expression of feelings, actions and outcomes, trust... as much as we can, even as gentle reminders such as &quot;10 more minutes then turning it off&quot;.</p>\n<p>When they get caught out doing the wrong thing, and questioned about it, &quot;why are you doing the wrong thing&quot;, they simply repeat back what we have discussed in the past, which means they do listen, but they are not following through on their behaviour and actions; self control bring the current and ongoing issue.</p>\n<p>This has all been going on and growing over 4 months, and is reaching the point where my partner and I are looking for alternate solutions, beyond simply punishment or different allowances or complete restiction, alternative activities (the younger one loves lego, and skateboarding, which we greatly encourage; the elder one reads quite a lot and has miniatures that he builds and paints, and we have introduced him to a gaming club so he has company with these activities).</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","father","play-time"],"owner":{"account_id":29144765,"reputation":173,"user_id":45888,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zLCII.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jan_B","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45888/jan-b"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":841,"accepted_answer_id":44124,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1713538328,"creation_date":1713362861,"question_id":44122,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44122/rough-and-tumble-play-with-1-5-yo-son-turns-to-actual-voilent-behavior","title":"Rough and tumble play with 1.5 yo son turns to actual voilent behavior","body":"<p>I have the feeling that my one-and-a-half-year-old son often wants to play wildly in the evenings or even have a kind of tussle. However, this almost exclusively follows the pattern that it is initially a kind of tickling and light tossing around, but after a few minutes it turns into violent scratching, nipping and biting on his part. He laughs at first, but the mood quickly changes when I make it clear that it hurts me and that I won't continue playing like this.</p>\n<p>My son is very cuddly and very focussed on my wife. She has a much calmer way of playing with him. I'm not at home much on weekdays and of course most of the time with my children consists of watching books or playing quietly. In addition to roughhousing, he also occasionally demands affectionate closeness, although he mainly seeks this from my wife, as he is also breastfed in the evening. I don't see this problem with my two years older daughter, who also seems more calm and prefers things to be less wild.</p>\n<p>I realise that children of this age like to test boundaries and are not yet capable of much empathy, but I still wonder whether it is possible to teach the boundaries between friendly roughhousing and actually hurting without overtaxing the child. Or would it be better to avoid playing too rough altogether?</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","adhd"],"owner":{"account_id":31629218,"reputation":11,"user_id":45892,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/57cc494bd19eac227fe8436a659f5b5f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Allie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45892/allie"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":626,"answer_count":0,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1780242315,"creation_date":1713395658,"last_edit_date":1713437549,"question_id":44123,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44123/6-year-old-aggressive-and-impulsive-behavior","title":"6 year old aggressive and impulsive behavior","body":"<p>My 6 year old stepson has shown signs of ADHD for many years. The worst of it is shown in school, where the behaviors are out of control. He has extra help and support from staff and teachers who give him breaks throughout the day. However nothing works. He has gotten suspended from school over 15 times, they have gotten to the point of possibly kicking him out. The suspensions are due to him biting, pushing, hitting and choking other children.</p>\n<p>We have tried behavior charts, reward system, time outs, extra breaks out of structured time.  We limit the stuff he watches and plays with and he's never been around or seen any violent things to where he would pick up this behavior. This behavior of aggressiveness only seems to be a problem around his classmates because he does not show aggression to us or older adults. We understand he struggled with impulsive control and ADHD so we try to give him extra support and understanding.</p>\n<p>I’m not sure what to do anymore. We have tried getting him into a program for children who struggle with ADHD, but they say he’s too young. We are on the waitlist for a therapist and many other programs when a spot opens up. We do discipline him such as taking away toys and not doing fun things and not allowing screen time, but I’m not sure how much this works because I don’t think his mother follows through with the same routine we do. We have also tried other methods of supplements and gummies to clam his body but these only do so much.</p>\n"},{"tags":["discipline","conflict","lying","hitting"],"owner":{"account_id":31704658,"reputation":21,"user_id":45904,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/sVvwY.jpg?s=256","display_name":"jellyfish skitty","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45904/jellyfish-skitty"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1037,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1715066013,"creation_date":1713617156,"last_edit_date":1713716231,"question_id":44129,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44129/5-year-old-wants-to-lie-hide-things-destroy-others-stuff-and-is-only-nice-to","title":"5 year old wants to lie, hide things, destroy others stuff, and is only nice to people when he gets what he wants","body":"<p>My 5 year old has been acting out terribly and I’m at a loss. My husband isn’t helping at all with parenting anymore for this one (the 5 year old his his) or the other two kids.</p>\n<p>The youngest is the one I’ve been having the most issues with: wanting to lie, hide things, destroy others properties, hit, and throws the worst tantrums if he doesn’t get what he wants. I understand he’s 5 so tantrums are a given no matter what, but the way he does it concerns me. He immediately wants to attack people hit them at very specific spots (head, eyes, face in general.)</p>\n<p>When he lies he has this hiding spot that me and husband know where it is immediately but he keeps hiding things he’s destroyed or had accidents and hides his underwear there.</p>\n<p>The only time he’s nice to others is when he has a screen. The tablet is like the golden ring to Gollum; if it’s gone his entire personality is different. I’ve had to resort to taking away his tablet and PlayStation time due to his behavior, and been doing it for a month and he’s still the same.</p>\n<p>I keep trying to talk to him see if there’s something going on at his mothers when he goes there or if school is bothering him, to see if that’s why he’s acting out but he just glares at me and screams till he’s blue in the face. Husband says he just has authority issues that the middle and eldest did the same thing that he will straighten out when he’s 7. But waiting it out isn’t ok, he can’t keep doing this. He’s going to think it’s ok to do it. I’m so lost here, I don’t believe in spanking at all and that’s all my mother tells me to do.</p>\n"},{"tags":["development","play-time","play","fighting","imaginative-play"],"owner":{"account_id":28130122,"reputation":51,"user_id":45243,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AGNmyxYpVixgSJU1iUrkcEwXXfHK70-PY3-0FUbdM2SK=k-s256","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45243/joe"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":59,"closed_date":1714032319,"answer_count":0,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1713994949,"creation_date":1713994949,"question_id":44131,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44131/is-this-a-safe-game-to-play-with-my-kids","closed_reason":"Duplicate","title":"Is this a safe game to play with my kids?","body":"<p>I play with my kids &quot;swords&quot;, they take a wooden ruler and I take nunchucks, I tell them that I am &quot;Bruce Lee&quot;, I don't strike them and I just avoid their attack with my nunchucks, when they hit my hips, or my belly several times - I fall on the floor on my back, and the kids say: &quot;daddy is dead...&quot;</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","education"],"owner":{"account_id":7149471,"reputation":2204,"user_id":20507,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/92b4eb27bd74129464d3d272476d8022?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Stu W","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/20507/stu-w"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":280,"accepted_answer_id":44134,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1714270194,"creation_date":1714245280,"last_edit_date":1714270194,"question_id":44133,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44133/advanced-placement-and-honors-classes","title":"Advanced Placement and Honors classes","body":"<p>This is a good problem to have….</p>\n<p>My daughter is an advanced learner. Her freshman year of high school, her <strong>lowest</strong> grade is 97%. She can start taking Honors classes next year and AP classes as a junior. Although I think AP classes are great for college credit, I have hesitation about Honors.</p>\n<p>Her HS does not weight the courses, so it’s the same 4.0 for an A in an Honors course compared to a non-Honors course also receiving a 4.0. Her likelihood of straight As in Honors (or more than two APs) is greatly diminished.</p>\n<p>So, <strong>Should I let my daughter breeze through HS, graduating potentially as valedictorian and having full ride scholarship offers OR encouraging her to be more challenged at school which would undoubtedly alter her class rank?</strong> It is of course possible that she could join the Honors program and still score straight As.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","social-development","anxiety","separation"],"owner":{"account_id":31953920,"reputation":21,"user_id":45934,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/3a5899f6a97180c211212864a353afb7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Charlie Bey","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45934/charlie-bey"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":417,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1715034259,"creation_date":1714372210,"question_id":44136,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44136/2-year-old-with-social-anxiety","title":"2 year old with social anxiety","body":"<p>I am losing sleep over my two year old girl. She has recently, in the past month or so, became very clingy to me. We send her to a weekly kinder music class which she used to enjoy, but now she won’t leave my arms when we get there. She will not participate no matter how much encouragement we give her.</p>\n<p>She does not interact with the other kids except for an older girl. My daughter is also not keen on toys offered, whereby other kids gets excited and just get amongst it!</p>\n<p>I’ve brought her to playgrounds, indoor playlands with slides and ball pits.. nothing seems to excite her. She will want me to hold her hand whilst she throws a few balls around, but that’s about all the involvement that she wants. Is this even normal?! Help!</p>\n<p>Additional info: at home she’s very social, interacts with neighbors (adults with pets) and could have basic conversations even. She has formed simple sentences of up to 7 words in English and even a few Chinese words.</p>\n"},{"tags":["motivation"],"owner":{"account_id":12902,"reputation":203,"user_id":7808,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b02cab91fb3c5604163c116c494de2a5?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Nathan H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7808/nathan-h"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":280,"answer_count":2,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1714562005,"creation_date":1714488666,"question_id":44138,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44138/force-9-year-old-to-finish-what-he-started","title":"Force 9-year old to finish what he started?","body":"<p>A few months ago, I subscribed my 9 year old son to a code learning platform for kids, to try to give him a taste for logic and technology.\nDuring that same period, he asked me for a game console.\nI took the opportunity and told him if he finished the entire coding course, he’d get a console.\nAt first, he was excited and kept asking for more. Little by little the tasks became more difficult and he played less and less. And the less he practiced, the more difficult it became. Now he’s really not enjoying it and gets frustrated a lot.</p>\n<p>Now, he asked me if I could give him a different challenge to get his game console.\nI’m hesitant:\nOn one hand I want to respect that he’s not obligated to like the things I like, and don’t want to force him.\nOn the other hand, I don’t want him to quit things in life just when they get a little difficult.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","sleep"],"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":582,"answer_count":2,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1715823695,"creation_date":1715127450,"last_edit_date":1715174411,"question_id":44146,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44146/what-to-do-in-the-moment-when-a-toddler-falls-asleep-so-late-for-his-nap-that-it","title":"What to do in the moment when a toddler falls asleep so late for his nap that it folds into his bedtime?","body":"<p>It happens sometimes - a 32 month old toddler who just doesn't want to sleep during the day and winds up being fully awake right up until an hour or so <em>before</em> he's supposed to go to bed.</p>\n<p>I've seen plenty of advice for how to fix a sleep schedule <em>after</em> such an event occurs - which is well and fine, but what I need more than anything in a situation like that is to know what to do <em>in the moment</em> when he's overslept his nap.</p>\n<p>Do I wake him up and try to keep him up for a little while so he isn't waking up too early?  Or do I let him sleep and accept that tomorrow will be a nightmare of possibly waking up hours before he's supposed to?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","behavior","violence"],"owner":{"account_id":233694,"reputation":153,"user_id":19202,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Ocgik.png?s=256","display_name":"Paula_plus_plus","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/19202/paula-plus-plus"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":360,"accepted_answer_id":44152,"answer_count":1,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1715378229,"creation_date":1715249086,"question_id":44150,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44150/is-my-toddler-prone-to-violence","title":"Is my toddler prone to violence?","body":"<p>I have a 2.5yo son who, from my point of view, finds violence amusing. Examples of this behaviour are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When my partner and me play with him, he likes to slap or punch us bursting into laughter if he succeeds.</li>\n<li>When we play with him using puppets or stuffed animals, he likes to slap or punch them, laughing when lands a hit or when we dodge the attack (as if he enjoys the chase).</li>\n<li>He laughs when on TV there's some act that implies suffering or damage (i.e. animals hunting on a documentary, Batwheels or Hotwheels cars crashing, Pocoyo's Duck falling or being hit by something, …).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As far as we know, the boy does not attack kindergarden classmates nor is he violent towards adults he is not used to. I wonder if this is our fault for making &quot;violence&quot; a game or is something common for boys on his age.</p>\n<p>Any clues or advise?</p>\n"},{"tags":["potty-training","autism"],"owner":{"account_id":20316533,"reputation":853,"user_id":40190,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gj5plYtcDIQpzM4Ol69AqnbTAojkE1SQnzn7PbH9A=k-s256","display_name":"Isabella Leonarda","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40190/isabella-leonarda"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":220,"accepted_answer_id":44156,"answer_count":1,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1715678031,"creation_date":1715431870,"last_edit_date":1715678031,"question_id":44155,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44155/potty-training-sibling-of-an-autistic-child","title":"potty training sibling of an autistic child","body":"<p>We have two kids (in 8 mos, we will have three). Our daughter is 4, diagnosed autistic. Our son is 2.5--never assessed, but does not appear to be autistic (displays no visible to us symptoms).</p>\n<p>While we lived in NY, due to state budget issues, she had no access to services until this past Jan (and it wasn't even full services that she was supposed to receive), so we had no prof assistance with her and her behavior was very difficult (she was unresponsive to most direction and would routinely lash out, especially at her brother).</p>\n<p>Despite this, over the course of about a year, we got her to pee in her potty. Her brother, whom we began training around same time, achieved same degree of success of peeing in potty. A month ago, we moved from NY to Illinois. The move severely shocked our daughter, and the first two weeks, she was tremendously combative and physically aggressive, especially with her brother. She hardcore refused to use the potty and we decided not to wage that battle. Two weeks ago, she was admitted into the school system and at last, began to receive full services. Her behavior improved and we are resuming potty training. Unfortunately, not only are we at ground zero with her on potty training, but also with him, as during our first two weeks here, while she was aggressive, he too began to refuse using the potty. So we are starting from scratch with him as well.</p>\n<p>My question is this: should we attempt to,\neither on our own or with professional assistance, speed track the potty training process with him (regardless of her rate of progress)\nor\nis it better to go back to the beginning and let it take how long it takes?</p>\n<p>Any additional thoughts appreciated.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","autism","aggression"],"owner":{"account_id":20316533,"reputation":853,"user_id":40190,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gj5plYtcDIQpzM4Ol69AqnbTAojkE1SQnzn7PbH9A=k-s256","display_name":"Isabella Leonarda","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40190/isabella-leonarda"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":621,"accepted_answer_id":44162,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1716377074,"creation_date":1715737046,"question_id":44159,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44159/optimal-way-to-handle-autistic-childs-aggression-against-sibling","title":"optimal way to handle autistic child&#39;s aggression against sibling?","body":"<p>We have two kids, a 4yr old girl, diagnosed autistic, and a 2.5 yr old boy, never assessed but unlikely to be autistic. Our daughter, who has recently begun receiving services, lashes out a few times a week at her brother (kicking/scratching/pushing) when she's overstimulated/not getting what she wants immediately. She's drawn blood by her attack of him.\nWe usually try to restrain and raise our voices at her as quickly as possible to prevent her causing him\nany serious damage. Unfortunately, we now see our son mimic her behavior, albeit nowhere to same degree.</p>\n<p>Should we pursue any particular approach to quell her outbursts that could, ideally, not be indicative of aggression (so that our son does not perceive our behavior as similar to hers)\nor\nis this something requiring professional intervention beyond what she receives at school?</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-teen","mental-health"],"owner":{"account_id":3690277,"reputation":741,"user_id":39807,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/WREQP.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Ben","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39807/ben"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":397,"answer_count":2,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1719452800,"creation_date":1716004835,"last_edit_date":1716040698,"question_id":44173,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44173/adressing-introducing-autism","title":"Adressing/introducing Autism","body":"<p>So my 10yo son has recently been diagnosed with functioning autism. It has been a big relief for us to actually understand what he is experiencing - he would pace a lot,  very fixated on destructive thoughts as entertaining, (&quot;and then imagine if it just blew up!&quot;), would get agitated seemingly out of nowhere, etc. It was frustrating for everyone because no one knew what the issues were, and he would be unable to explain. Now that we understand, we can then solve the issues.</p>\n<p>However what I am struggling with is telling him that he has autism. One of his biggest habits is latching on to labels. He would use them as excuses for behaviours. For example dealing with self control, he would not fix the self control, regardless of the direction, and motivation we might give him, address and readdress, he would simply blame the &quot;lack of self control&quot;.</p>\n<p>In the same way I don't want him to identify himself as autistic, and use that as a label by which to limit himself.</p>\n<p>For example, I myself have epilepsy. However it is not something I use to identify myself, it's just a part of my life. I take medication, I avoid triggers and I keep aware of any potential syndromes, but otherwise I live my life as per normal. I do not limit myself by my epilepsy.</p>\n<p>How can I address this with my son, in a way that he understands that he is not an autistic person, but a person that has autism?</p>\n"},{"tags":["discipline","potty-training"],"owner":{"account_id":20316533,"reputation":853,"user_id":40190,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14Gj5plYtcDIQpzM4Ol69AqnbTAojkE1SQnzn7PbH9A=k-s256","display_name":"Isabella Leonarda","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40190/isabella-leonarda"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":211,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1716093616,"creation_date":1716079385,"question_id":44174,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44174/non-militant-approach-to-potty-training","title":"non-militant approach to potty training","body":"<p>I have heard a variety of styles of potty-training and the ones I have been told of struck me as somewhat militant. This is not to say that discipline is something I want to ignore in my kids' lives, but, for example, one mother told me how she trained her kids: she left them naked and refused to leave the house or clothe them until they used the potty. She insisted that method worked, but it struck me as extremely coercive. I could be wrong, but this style is very unappealing to me.\nOur 4yr old daughter is autistic and non-verbal. Coercion of this sort would absolutely backfire. Our 2.5 yr old is not autistic but I am unsure of such an approach would be great for him.</p>\n<p>Are there non-coercive methods that may take longer, but consider the child's free will to some extent? Or is that an unrealistic expectation?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","behavior"],"owner":{"account_id":32382785,"reputation":31,"user_id":46018,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/260b6b749485a290d2f04a520a25ca0f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Jeru John","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/46018/jeru-john"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1853,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1719361625,"creation_date":1716234244,"last_edit_date":1717873106,"question_id":44178,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44178/is-it-normal-for-my-2-year-old-to-to-sleep-with-his-grandmother-and-refuse-to-sl","title":"Is it normal for my 2 year old to to sleep with his grandmother and refuse to sleep with his parents?","body":"<p>Is it normal for my 2 year old son to sleep with his grandmother and refuse to sleep with his parents? I feel that I am not comforting my child enough and that's why he is refusing to sleep with us.</p>\n"},{"tags":["parents"],"owner":{"account_id":32386925,"reputation":19,"user_id":46019,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocL0C04oR_oRMOFbXxCzzpDEjhymn6exQeXkfDo4M-jeCFWIsDZZ=k-s256","display_name":"Mikey Watkins","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/46019/mikey-watkins"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":447,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1716371870,"creation_date":1716257743,"last_edit_date":1716371870,"question_id":44179,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44179/my-4-year-old-daughter-does-not-know-im-her-dad-and-her-mom-refuses-to-tell-her","title":"My 4 year old daughter does not know im her dad and her mom refuses to tell her","body":"<p>I will try to be as neutral as possible with the situation, my significant other, and I had a daughter and during the first two years of her life, I was experiencing mental health issues and homelessness, and was absent from her life.</p>\n<p>Me and my significant other ended up getting back together, and we slowly started introducing our daughter to me as she has never really been around a man before. She didn’t want to tell her at first that I was her dad because it didn’t want to and these are her words freak her out all the sudden she has a dad. So for a whole entire year, I kind of sat back and just let the relationship with my daughter be fluid and grow on her and become close with her so I can eventually tell her that I’m her dad, but mom has been so against it.</p>\n<p>We fell in a rough spot and I ended up losing my job and mom lost her job and we lost our apartment so our daughter moved in with her grandmother, my significant other rented a room from a family member that wouldn’t allow me to be there so now my daughter is at her grandmas and I haven’t seen her or talk to her in seven months. My significant other ended up losing her job and then she had to move in with me in the room that I’m renting, but she still will not let me see our daughter. She will not let me talk to her on the phone.</p>\n<p>They don’t bring me up in conversation  or acknowledge that I’m asking about her every day trying to see her buying her gifts. I’m paying her daycare. I am financially supporting my daughter, but I do not get to see her and she still has no idea that I’m her father.</p>\n<p>I have a full-time job, mentally healthy and I am providing as those were the requirements for me to be in my daughters life, but they are still not letting me see her. They will not tell her that I am her biological father. They do not tell her what her last name is she goes by her grandmother‘s last name which is not on her birth certificate it’s my last name, I’m just a total loss. I honestly wanna go behind my significant others back and just go to court. idk what to do please help.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","fears","anxiety"],"owner":{"account_id":6950583,"reputation":555,"user_id":35501,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Hivf3.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Kjara","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/35501/kjara"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":556,"answer_count":1,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1717434627,"creation_date":1716895022,"last_edit_date":1716901759,"question_id":44185,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44185/1-5-year-old-afraid-of-everything-after-an-accident","title":"1.5 year old afraid of everything after an accident","body":"<p>I had an accident with my 1.5 year old daughter two days ago: I carried her in my carrier on the chest and, while trying to move a bulky item through a narrow opening, I stumbled so that she was tilted towards the side and hit her head quite hard on an iron gate next to us. She cried and I tried to comfort her immediately. She did not cry for long, less than 5 minutes, and for the rest of the day I did not notice anything strange. We went to bed. In the night, she woke up very often, crying, so at some point I took her into my bed. In the early morning of yesterday, everything seemed still well and normal.</p>\n<p>But then we went outside to run errands and there it started: She looked mostly down while walking, which is an unusual behaviour for her. Then she stopped and pointed at the ants on the ground. I told her some things about the ants, we watched them, and then I tried to make her come with me. She did not want to, she seemed troubled. Then a fire bug (much larger than an ant) walked towards her and she started crying and tried to move her feet upwards (yes, both) so that it was clear to me that she was afraid of that bug. So I took her into my arms. Tried to tell her some interesting things about fire bugs, pointed at them, moved a little closer, but she had enough and just wanted to be away from it. Okay, I carried her the rest of the way. Whenever I had to put her down (to get stuff out of my pocket etc.), she looked down and seemed to be scanning for dangers on the ground.</p>\n<p>Next issue: After the afternoon nap we went outside to go to the playground. She wanted to walk down the stairs on her own, as usual. Again, she looked down, scanning for dangers. Then she found one: a pebble stone on the stair. She refused to walk any further. She tried going around it, but failed (there was no handrail on the other side so she could not take the step) and just stood there. I tried to show her how interesting that pebble stone was, touched it, walked on it, showed here that it is not dangerous. Didn't help. So I carried her again until outside.</p>\n<p>On the playground she accepted all the suggestions I gave her (things she usually likes or loves, like &quot;Do you want to swing?&quot;, &quot;Let's bake a sand cake!&quot;), but she was not fully there. I mean mentally. She was absent somehow and could not really enjoy it. In the sand I noticed that she tried to get rid of every bit of sand on her hands all the time.</p>\n<p>During dinner, she was mostly normal again (we found something to make her laugh again before dinner, so her mood seemed to be good), but like in the sand, whenever she spilled something (even if just a little drop of tea or a bread crumb), she wanted it cleaned.</p>\n<p>The night I let her sleep with me from the beginning. She cried often in the night, but did not seem to wake up (just some cries, then silence again, eyes closed all the time).</p>\n<p>Today I went to the doctor with her to have a look at the bump on her head. Was afraid that I damaged something. But the doctor said they have no reason to believe that there is any long-term physical damage (like bone fracture or brain damage): She did not vomit, she did not pass out, she did not behave lethargic for almost 48 hours after the accident. Her pupils and reflexes are all normal, and the bump is not very large.</p>\n<p>So let's assume the strange behaviour is &quot;merely&quot; psychological. I can imagine that the incident is/was very hard on her psychologically: She was in the most comfortable, most secure/safe situation she knows (carried by me in the carrier). And then she got hit so hard out of nothing. She could not even see anything coming because her face was towards me, she was hit on the back of her head. I know that such things - experiencing something unexpected and painful in a situation that is associated with complete security/safety - can destroy confidence in life, at least for adults. So why not for children as well?</p>\n<p>So I just have one question: <strong>What can I do to help her get over it?</strong></p>\n<p>At her age, she has not enough verbal skills to understand me if I explain to her what happened.</p>\n<p>I have to note that I myself had an anxiety disorder as a child (started around age 10, slowly faded away around 18), and that increased need for order and cleanliness (like with the sand on her hands and spilled food during dinner) is a clear sign of stress in my case. So she may have gotten all the ingredients, genetically, to develop an anxiety disorder herself.</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","safety"],"owner":{"account_id":22227260,"reputation":557,"user_id":45224,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJwzcgOLTOqjclf1YW1S8sz5SxiWAIY191kUd2-r=k-s256","display_name":"multitaskingmom","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/45224/multitaskingmom"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":260,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1717422529,"creation_date":1717378062,"question_id":44187,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44187/children-bike-riding-on-wet-sidewalk","title":"Children Bike Riding on Wet Sidewalk","body":"<p>Is it safe to allow my children to ride their bikes/scooters on a wet sidewalk?</p>\n<p>I can't recall from my childhood whether wet sidewalks are more slippery.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","pre-schooler"],"owner":{"account_id":32728151,"reputation":31,"user_id":46084,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/4c8868e156926f172c184f0858b77a6d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user46084","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/46084/user46084"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":398,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1719624186,"creation_date":1717614005,"last_edit_date":1719624186,"question_id":44194,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44194/ohio-what-are-my-options-to-put-my-4-year-old-daughter-to-preschool","title":"OHIO - What are my options to put my 4 year old daughter to Preschool?","body":"<p>Here is my situation: I moved to this country 3 months ago. I wasn't aware how schools work in this country. I'm still learning about the school system. My kid is turning 4 years old this month. My kid is not a native English speaker.</p>\n<p>I called the county school system, and they said they are full. I looked at a nearby church school and they are full as well? What can I do? I feel like me being &quot;lazy&quot; about it has made it bad for my kid.</p>\n<p>Could you please suggest something?</p>\n"},{"tags":["sleep-training","night-time","cosleeping","nightwaking","sleep-deprivation"],"owner":{"account_id":32794498,"reputation":31,"user_id":46122,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocJSU1P6dSOpVd1UzmeFIk-uy906K5_3A8YvBc8pIx0FBMNKyg=k-s256","display_name":"Muskan Bhardwaj","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/46122/muskan-bhardwaj"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":792,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1718007032,"creation_date":1717884281,"question_id":44197,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44197/why-does-my-14-month-old-baby-wakes-up-crying-asking-for-water-and-banana-in-the","title":"Why does my 14 month old baby wakes up crying asking for water and banana in the night","body":"<p>I exclusively breastfed my baby and he sleeps with us. Needs my touch. Wakes up 3-4 times. 3 times for water once for banana now it is a habit.</p>\n"},{"tags":["language-development","bilingual"],"owner":{"account_id":25909704,"reputation":121,"user_id":46271,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/8eb85630c1ee3a2b3fb76036da71dee5?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"D.J. Elkind","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/46271/d-j-elkind"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":358,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1732684854,"creation_date":1718445966,"question_id":44205,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44205/handbook-for-intermediary-level-esl-to-talk-to-babies-in-english","title":"Handbook for intermediary level ESL to talk to babies in English","body":"<p>My wife is an intermediary ESL speaker (she didn't take any standardized test, I guess her level is ~B1-B2). She is now interacting with our ~one-year old baby ~90% in mother tongue and ~10% in English.</p>\n<p>I am now exploring ways to increase the portion of English use. I am wondering if anyone can recommend some books to teach adults(i.e., my wife) to communicate with kids in English. The book should be about practical language use, not parenting theories. For example, the book should explain (to ESL parents) how to say &quot;we need to change your diaper&quot; / &quot;let's take a nap now&quot; / &quot;Be quiet on a bus&quot;/etc.</p>\n<p>Any recommendations?</p>\n"},{"tags":["screen-time","cellphone"],"owner":{"account_id":359310,"reputation":413,"user_id":42695,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/36sla.png?s=256","display_name":"Sam7919","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42695/sam7919"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":647,"answer_count":2,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1719235751,"creation_date":1718568787,"last_edit_date":1718723819,"question_id":44207,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44207/what-is-a-reasonable-solution-to-screen-time-evasion","title":"What is a reasonable solution to screen-time evasion?","body":"<p>I really didn't expect this outcome. I expected &quot;<em>one hour of screen-time</em>&quot; to mean just that. The child learns to moderate the time spent on an iOS smartphone over waking hours.</p>\n<p>In practice the child quickly uses up the hour of screen-time, and then continues indefinitely using the iPhone.</p>\n<p>It turns out that there is a loophole. &quot;<em>One more minute</em>&quot; is available in each app. I have yet to figure out how it's used. It's not the case, for example, that an additional minute can be obtained for the same app, but perhaps it's possible to flip-flop between a pair of apps (games), or it's possible to uninstall then reinstall the same app.</p>\n<p>That a child is willing to go through that much trouble to evade screen-time is by itself puzzling.</p>\n<p>What is a reasonable solution to screen-time evasion?</p>\n<p>I'm contemplating:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>withdrawing iOS and providing a dumb phone (and dealing with the child's wrath, plus endless comparisons with peers);</li>\n<li>replacing iOS with Android, which doesn't seem to have this loophole (oddly, this is more expensive, because of the ability to recycle a gently used device but one with otherwise good specs); or</li>\n<li>somehow convincing the child to abandon what starts to look like an addiction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Have you been there? Did you find a good solution?</p>\n<h4>Note 1</h4>\n<p>What the operating system provides can quickly change. I have been having this problem  with iOS releases up to and including iOS 17.</p>\n<h4>Note 2</h4>\n<p>iOS has an an additional constraint to refuse access during bedtime. Yet one additional setting enables one or more apps to be used regardless of the bedtime or screen-time constraints — notably to be able to call or send a message (seeking help, a ride, etc — the primary function for having a phone in the first place). Neither of these directly solves the main problem described above.</p>\n<h4>Note 3</h4>\n(Added after BenjyTec's answer.)\n<p>I had already enabled &quot;Block at Downtime&quot; and &quot;Block at End of Limit&quot;. They do not help.</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","food","lying","junkfood"],"owner":{"account_id":33157849,"reputation":91,"user_id":46538,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cdea810ca591debee91295424ef9aaeb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Kisha","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/46538/kisha"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":13844,"answer_count":5,"score":9,"last_activity_date":1719433777,"creation_date":1719184833,"last_edit_date":1719433777,"question_id":44211,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44211/my-5-year-old-is-stealing-food-and-lying","title":"My 5-year-old is stealing food and lying","body":"<p>My 5-year-old has begun stealing food and lying about it. Most of the time, he takes sweets. Therefore, in my household, we limit the amount of sweets he may have. If he does not receive it, he waits until we are not looking and then takes it.</p>\n<p>I usually check on my children between the hours of 6 a.m. and 7 a.m. When I went to my 5-year-old's room, he had a whole candy and cookies stash under his pillow. I talked to him, and he's still doing it. Not sure what's going on, I feel like I'm failing as a mom.</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","teen","rules"],"owner":{"account_id":33172951,"reputation":21,"user_id":46561,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/866beb4d9702471ae1e92d5b3e2f6222?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"katherine","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/46561/katherine"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1564,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1719566703,"creation_date":1719236441,"last_edit_date":1719325446,"question_id":44215,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44215/how-to-deal-with-unreasonable-strict-parents","title":"How to deal with unreasonable strict parents?","body":"<p>I’m turning 16 in a little over a month. Since I started high school I actually started making friends but can’t make any plans with. I stay at home every miserable day, doing the same thing.</p>\n<p>My dad dislikes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>how much I use my phone, but what else is there to do if I do chores &amp; do my dual enrollment work?</li>\n<li>my boy friend (I haven’t even told him I'm dating my boy friend, do I have pictures of him on phone and on the wall in my room)\n<ul>\n<li>mom also told him about my boy friend and said he was a good kid who had good grades &amp; was very respectful &amp; my dad said that he was going to be watching my behavior and how my grades were once I got back to school (I never slacked off so bad that I was failing. I got A’s B’s &amp; C’s this sophomore year and my final grades were all mostly B’s)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>finding out I'm doing something 'stupid' or that I'm hiding something from him</li>\n<li>my friends, who he believes are useless self centered kids and get to do whatever they want</li>\n</ul>\n<p>My dad's rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>hand phone over at 9pm everyday, including weekends</li>\n<li>curfew at 9pm</li>\n<li>can't go out more than 2-3 hours</li>\n<li>no door, instead have blackout curtain so any time I'm in my room I can be monitored and checked in on</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I've tried talking to my mom for some type of understanding but it’s always back and forth. She understands me but doesn’t. I've been working and trying my best to fulfill whatever they put towards me. I started being a lot more responsible because I need to bring up the fact I have a boy friend to my dad &amp; I don’t want an excuse for it to be a no like me getting bad grades or not doing enough around the house. I asked my mom a few days ago if I could go watch Inside Out 2 &amp; she said she would take me this week since my dad will be out of town because while he’s in town we can’t do anything extra. She said she was going to take me so we could watch it but I didn’t want to watch it with her. The whole idea was for me to go with my bestfriend, her boy friend &amp; my boy friend.</p>\n<p>Everything I've had to do so far to even get to maintain my relationship with my boy friend is sneak around. During school I would leave school to spend more time with him or I just wouldn’t go into school at all just so we could actually do what we wanted to like get food or just be able to watch a movie at the comfort of his house. I can’t go out, we have a time limit, everything is rushed because “what if he calls”.  My mom told me my dad wouldn’t let me hang out with my boy friend like that even though he’s out of town. She said she’s trying to be chill about the fact I told her me &amp; my boy friend got intimate like what did you expect? She got me pills herself to use as contraceptives because she assumes me and my boy friend are going to keep being intimate but she doesn’t expect me to wanna ask to see him. I got frustrated and started crying. She felt bad and said she understood me and she was going to let me know sometime this week to tell him to get ready so we could go to a park.</p>\n<p>It’s been 18 days since I last saw him &amp; I know it’s not a big deal but my boy friend and bestfriend are the only people I actually find comfort in. Me not being able to see him has been putting me in such a corner &amp; I feel bad for even keeping him in the midst of this that I have going on. Now the excuse this week is that since my sister needs the car that means I'll have to stay inside yet again this whole week and not get to see my boy friend.</p>\n<p>What I'd like to do:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>meet my boy friend's family, as I've been invited. My boy friend has offered to let his mom talk to my parents</li>\n<li>go out more, especially during summer\n<ul>\n<li>sleepover with friends, at least my best friend</li>\n<li>go to the beach with boy friend and group of friends</li>\n<li>go to movies and eat out after</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>be un-chaperoned by parents</li>\n<li>not have plans cancelled last minute by parents</li>\n<li>being happy for the flower I bring home from my boy friend</li>\n<li>fewer unnecessary comments</li>\n<li>maybe being emancipated, don't think the court will accept the proof I have/running away\n<ul>\n<li>neither my boyfriend or I are 16, so we can't get jobs</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Please give me some advice if any. I sometimes can rely on my sister and spend the weekend at her house but as of right now I can’t as she’s wanting to move and is packing. I feel so drained out though &amp; the worst part about it is that I've been getting lately more emotional so everything has been taking a toll on me because I'm sensitive. I have nowhere to look to or go. I don’t know what to do.</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","discipline","parents","tantrums"],"owner":{"account_id":482697,"reputation":4291,"user_id":13387,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/efdd0371ee7b983ab3b858ea92653aeb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"dsollen","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13387/dsollen"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":561,"answer_count":2,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1720074842,"creation_date":1719340284,"last_edit_date":1719500631,"question_id":44219,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44219/should-i-bother-trying-to-teach-my-niece-i-wont-respond-to-tantrums-if-i-only-s","title":"Should I bother trying to teach my niece I won&#39;t respond to tantrums if I only see her once a month and risk her mom undermining me if I don&#39;t give in","body":"<p>I get to see my niece roughly once a month.  Her mother, who is raising her, is not related to me, but we get along well enough.  Unfortunately the mother allows her daughter to get away with throwing tantrums to get what she wants.  My niece is generally a good kid usually and often cooperates with me, it's kind of random when she will happily go along with something and when a tantrum will happen.</p>\n<p>Now I have plenty of experience with different kids, and have dealt with tantrums before. I know how to handle tantrums and express to a child that I love them - even when they are throwing tantrums - but that I also won't give in to a tantrums when I'm officially in charge, I have plenty of practice with that.  My problem is that the mother is usually near by during my visits and will show up when she hears her daughter crying.  After that the daughter usually ends up getting what she wanted from Mom, meaning her tantrum still ended up working!</p>\n<p>To give an idea of frequency I spent half a day with her on Sunday and had to deal with two tantrums in that time.   In both cases I refused to give in, in one case Mom came to comfort my niece in the middle of the tantrum rewarding her fighting with extra attention.  The other time I just barely managed to get her to say sorry - I'd refused to continue our game until she apologized to her cousin which triggered the tantrum - but only by partially playing dumb with mom as to the cause of the tantrum, distracting Mom for a minute, and dumb luck that niece was just at the state where she was ready to reengage and thankfully didn't decide to keep fighting saying sorry when I asked for it a second time.  I felt like I was walking a tightrope trying to get a simple sorry out of my niece now that mom was around.</p>\n<p>I know kids can understand different adults have different rules, but if mom is usually close enough to come in and reward the tantrum when I refuse to I'm not sure my niece will learn she shouldn't try tantrums with me; after all tantrums still work via summoning mom. Can a 4 year old really be expected to learn and remember that I won't support tantrums if she sees me only once a month and my attempt to teach her are often interrupted by mom?</p>\n<p>I know how to handle tantrums with kids I only volunteer with and won't see regularly as well, I could better distract and defuse such a situation if I wasn't actively trying to teach my niece that she shouldn't try to use tantrums to get things out of me.  So I'm torn between using tantrums as a teaching moment to show her  that they won't work - which is what I'd prefer to do if it wasn't for my concern it won't work if mom is around- and giving up on teaching and simply resorting to defusing the situation as easily as I can (without fully giving in to the tantrum, I simple refuse to do that), so I can get back to having fun with her and making the most of my visits.</p>\n<p>Before anyone suggests it, mom is not open to parenting advice from me.  Talking with her about my concerns won't fix things, and could potentially risk alienating her and making it harder to get visits with my niece so it's not really something I want to risk.</p>\n"},{"tags":["middle-childhood","attention","self-control"],"owner":{"account_id":3690277,"reputation":741,"user_id":39807,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/WREQP.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Ben","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39807/ben"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":328,"answer_count":1,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1720540613,"creation_date":1719367554,"last_edit_date":1719380878,"question_id":44223,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44223/extending-consequences-to-deal-with-ongoing-behaviour","title":"&quot;Extending&quot; consequences to deal with ongoing behaviour","body":"<p>So my 10 yo is having self control issues, primarily to do with stealing (limited to treats, drinks etc) that do not belong to him, or controlling his own environment regardless of the effect it has on others.</p>\n<p>We have spoken to him about this behaviour, explaining from several different angles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Perspective of this behaviour happening to him instead; how would he feel if someone had taken something from him? Lollies he got from a party bag, or a drink he bought with his own money?</li>\n<li>Self-control is an active thought process; a decision to act one way or another. He has expressed an understanding of this, and has also explained that he is aware of the cause and effect of his behaviour during the action, but still decides to carry out the behaviour.</li>\n<li>Financial cost/reimbursement of the behaviours. He earns pocket money with jobs, and putting it simply, again form a different perspective is basically just giving that hard-earned money to someone else. He works for the money, only to have it taken from him.</li>\n<li>This also applies to controlling his environment (he turns on lights overnight as a &quot;night light&quot; - he does already have a night light in his room - in his room, in the hallway, and on the front porch outside his bedroom window when he can't sleep). This behaviour costs money, and if it continues (which it has) he will need to do extra work to compensate for the additional incurred costs. We have even pushed the potential of security risks of this, with little apparent impact.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our latest attempt to explain this (which we use for everyone in the house, including my partner and I) is the &quot;Three C's: Communication, Consideration, and Cooperation&quot; which basically boils down to: we talk to each other when there is conflict, expressing our feelings and what the problem(s) are; we consider each other's feelings; and we find a way to cooperate with each other.</p>\n<p>These behaviours have been ongoing for over 6 months, and when we believe there is an improvement in the behaviour, he gets caught out again (either by getting caught red-handed, or new food wrappers/hidden cups in his bedroom during a big clean).</p>\n<p>We have decided to give him a consequence of breaking up sticks/branches in the backyard, as well as restrictions to his time on iPads, watching TV etc, and does not get &quot;special treats&quot; unless we say so. He spends an hour each day in the yard, and we have explained that the duration of the punishment is up to him - the more work he does each time, the shorter the duration of the consequence. We allow him to do as he wishes however. If he chooses to simply sit around, that's his decision, though we do remind him of the obligation, and we do supervise to ensure he isn't doing anything he shouldn't be.</p>\n<p>However, he is still carrying out the misbehaviour. It is even becoming more frequent, and he does seem unperturbed by the consequences. We have stated that for each time this occurs, he will be adding an additional day to the consequence, but again, this seems to be having little impact. In truth, I believe that this may be becoming an attention seeking behaviour.</p>\n<p>His older brother that has different privileges (he has a laptop as a requirement for school) which he uses to play Minecraft with friends, and a phone to contact them. He also has different behaviours (he has invested in Warhammer miniatures, therefore he has a better interest in carrying out his jobs to earn pocket money) however he is also more introverted, but with changes in his social environments, as well as potential early teenage hormonal changes (he is only 12 so this may not be a factor) he has random, violent and emotional outbursts.</p>\n<p>He, on the other hand, is still only interested in Lego, and due to his excessive extroverted personality and behaviours (children in his class have expressed that he is &quot;very intense&quot;), he can be very attention seeking, and due to the fact that he doesn't have many people in his life to spend time with, this falls on his mother and I, and his older brother; which does quickly become overbearing.</p>\n<p>I am starting to believe that &quot;more consequences, more pressure, more strictness&quot; is not going to work, as I have explained that this is potentially becoming an attention-seeking behaviour, but the original approach of explanation has made no impact either. What can we do to help him correct his behaviours, without causing further conflict within the family?</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Some clarifications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>How much attention is he getting?</strong> As both of us work a 40-hour week, during this time it is mostly from his older brother (which, as I explained has been directed away from the younger brother to his friends), and his mother and I are generally either spending our own time relaxing, or dealing with cooking, cleaning etc. When it comes to chores, I tend to hover about and keep them both on task, giving compliments and encouragement where I can, particularly if tension is getting high from emotions, stress etc. During the weekends, Mum tries to come up with family activities, like going to the park or visiting family, and we often ask the older brother to spend time away from his devices to do things with us/his brother.\n<ul>\n<li>I do believe that due to his intensity - he often &quot;contributes&quot; to a lot of conversations that he is not a part of, or he continues a joke to the point of annoyance, or he jumps to very dark imagery as &quot;funny&quot;, probably due to scary/horror games (E.g. Five nights at Freddy's) being a fun topic. This causes us to ask him to stop a lot of the time, or calm down, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In regards to assistance, I am going to personal therapy, my partner has been in and out of therapy for herself, and we are looking into the boy's mental health as well. We have a potential that he may be brushing the spectrum, but we have yet to receive confirmation after an initial analysis. Both boys have been seeing counsellors which has been revealing for us (for example other kids seeing him as being too intense).</li>\n<li><strong>What are the major sources of conflict?</strong> Primarily emotions - what is &quot;fair&quot; and &quot;unfair&quot; from different perspectives. For example, after several instances of pointing out that a job has not been completed properly, explaining what needs to be done, how it was not achieved, and giving advice on how to do it, make it easy, etc., placing down a consequence for repeated lack of effort is immediately responded with &quot;not fair&quot; which can quickly escalate, especially on stressful days as management of this almost always falls back on me depending on how stressful the day has been for my partner.\n<ul>\n<li>In this instance, the only idea of &quot;correcting behaviour&quot; would be to remove any kind of &quot;treat&quot; from the house. However this could end in two ways - everyone feeling this is unfair, &quot;why should everyone suffer because of one person&quot;, which would likely punish him indirectly and even harder; or after a stint of inaccessibility, similar to the addiction conundrum of exposure to it causing the initial problem to escalate.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"tags":["sex"],"owner":{"account_id":33533007,"reputation":11,"user_id":46877,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e7f58fdec373e8e1df12aa76655774db?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"James","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/46877/james"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":437,"closed_date":1720120272,"answer_count":1,"score":-1,"last_activity_date":1720075844,"creation_date":1719983752,"last_edit_date":1720075844,"question_id":44231,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44231/my-wife-wants-to-have-sex-with-my-1-year-old-present-i-dont-she-gets-mad-at-me","closed_reason":"Not suitable for this site","title":"My wife wants to have sex with my 1 year old present, I dont. She gets mad at me, what can I do?","body":"<p>I have a one year old son that sleeps in the bed with me and my wife. When he's asleep she tries to have sex with me but I will not do it. I feel it's weird and I'm too busy looking at him trying to make sure he doesn't wake up and see us, it's just not cool.</p>\n<p>How can I best handle this situation?</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen","abuse"],"owner":{"account_id":33647968,"reputation":21,"user_id":46931,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b2808bdf401c18265e5ceb4eddd91df3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"OceanDog47","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/46931/oceandog47"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":105,"closed_date":1720254230,"answer_count":0,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1720127135,"creation_date":1720126682,"last_edit_date":1720127135,"question_id":44234,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44234/im-14-and-i-have-an-abusive-mom-what-do-i-do","closed_reason":"Duplicate","title":"Im 14 and i have an abusive mom what do i do?","body":"<p>(For people asking i live in iran) For all my life I've been abused by my mom (we are talking her yelling at me from as young as 3 to hitting me from around 4 to 5 years old until now which I am 14) she has abused me both emotionally and physically she keeps hitting me for saying &quot;no&quot; yeah she hits me just for saying no and I've gotten depression and ptsd from her hitting I have contemplated suicide but my dad has helped a lot in keeping me going (my mom also keeps yelling at home and sometimes hitting him) she has started to yell and hit my 6 (almost 7) year old sister and my dad hasent divorced my mom because of my sister and every time I try to talk to my mom about how what she is doing is bad she plays the victim and says that &quot;its all your dad's fault&quot; and &quot;you deserve it&quot; and so on and today she took my laptop and I put it down and she said il put it to your head as she walked away and I followed her and just looked at her while she kept cursing me and my dad and then I went and sit on the couch and waited then she took 2 cups from the dining table and asked my sister &quot;which one of theese cups are yours?&quot; And my sister said idk and then she asked me the same question and I was lying on the couch at this point and couldnt see the cups so I asked which cups and she said theese two cups (she didn't even raise the cups so I could see them) and I said which cups again and she said come look and I said I don't want to and she said if you take another cup il beat you up and I said (I don't know the exact translation from what i said to english but I think it's I'm doing good but the meaning is il do it again kind of) and she stomped her way to me and took off her slipper and was gonna hit me but I grabbed her hand and didn't let her and after struggling for a bit (it was mainly her struggling because I'm stronger) I let go and she hit me like 3 times and I said if you hit me again il fight back and she said (again idk the exact translation but it's like: you'll eat shit if you do) and she hit me again and then off I didn't hit her but I pinned her to the couch and put the part of my arm which starts at my elbow and ends at my wrist to her neck to incapacitate her (key word incapacitate not hurt) and she was pulling my hair and saying &quot;let your dirty hands go of me ungrateful shit&quot; and I let her go because the repairman we called was at the door and yeah it's night I'm taking a shower as I write this idk what to do since she will keep yelling and hitting me no doubt so I'm putting this here to maybe get some help.. thank you.</p>\n"},{"tags":["dogs"],"owner":{"account_id":5581116,"reputation":513,"user_id":28406,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b7530f691015a73111d9acf83baabd2a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Akron","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/28406/akron"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":5556,"accepted_answer_id":44240,"answer_count":5,"score":9,"last_activity_date":1720538136,"creation_date":1720387401,"question_id":44236,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44236/coping-with-consequences-of-a-dog-bite-before-buying-a-puppy","title":"Coping with consequences of a dog bite before buying a puppy","body":"<p>During our visit to the breeder, my daughter (8) was lightly bitten in the face by the mother dog (a certified Golden Retriever). It happened very unexpectedly; the dog had previously shown herself to be very friendly. The lip bled a bit, and there were long, superficial scratches on the nose. My daughter is brave, but the parents are somewhat frightened and unsettled by the injury to their child before their eyes and the experience of losing control.</p>\n<p>What is the correct responsible behavior for the parents? The daughter is very keen on having a dog, but the parents have learned that dogs can be unpredictable. Is this part of the &quot;residual risk&quot; that comes with having a dog?</p>\n"},{"tags":["siblings"],"owner":{"account_id":33887895,"reputation":101,"user_id":47053,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/97e4bcb7f999c544e69818cda5d7c92d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"NewToParenting","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/47053/newtoparenting"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":11669,"answer_count":9,"score":10,"last_activity_date":1721760380,"creation_date":1720636468,"question_id":44245,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44245/older-brother-licking-younger-sisters-legs","title":"Older brother licking younger sister&#39;s legs","body":"<p>Girlfriend and I have a daughter (will be 2 soon), and she has a son (7 turning 8 soon) from a previous marriage. Recently we have been butting heads a lot concerning a few behaviors of son towards daughter, and my reactions to them. One behavior in particular is son's continuing to touch daughter when she is yelling or crying for him to stop (usually she will yell 'no' repeatly, swing her arm at him, or try to push him away; given the size difference and her level of language skills, this is all she can do), and when being told by myself or by mother to leave her alone.</p>\n<p>Most recently there was an incident where daughter was lying on pillow in bed in her diaper, and son came in and got on bed and started messing with her. She was relaxing and didn't want to be bothered, so as frequently happens, she started saying no and trying to push him away from her. I didn't say anything immediately, because as mentioned, it is a frequent source of family discord, so I try to choose my battles. He then stuck out his tongue and began to lick her leg, between the knee and the hip, at which point I immediately firmly told him to stop and that he should not do that.</p>\n<p>Shortly after, when mother was back in the room, I brought it up with her and asked her to have a conversation with him, because I considered it inappropriate. She got angry at me and told me I only thought it was inappropriate because he was not my son, that if he was my son I would not have a problem with it; and because I was raised by strict, religious, prudish parents (which is true, although I have not been religious myself for decades now, and don't feel that I am prudish).</p>\n<p>My question is basically, is it normal or okay for a boy of that age to be licking his younger sister like that? Does it warrant a conversation with him about boundaries, respecting personal space and the desire to be left alone/not touched; or is it better to just ignore? Is it something that most parents would be comfortable with, and am I out of line being bothered by it?</p>\n"},{"tags":["discipline"],"owner":{"account_id":25884350,"reputation":19,"user_id":47134,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AItbvmmaf1y9f4Y9AK0BdWZ7WhhRWIliEwoTY5HN4ssx=k-s256","display_name":"Militant.Rectangle","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/47134/militant-rectangle"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":580,"closed_date":1723992257,"answer_count":3,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1723789886,"creation_date":1720636873,"last_edit_date":1720655996,"question_id":44246,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44246/how-do-you-reduce-the-anger-a-mother-feels-towards-her-child","closed_reason":"Not suitable for this site","title":"How do you reduce the anger a mother feels towards her child?","body":"<p>I met a woman and things have been going well. Recently she has introduced me to her family (older sister and a niece). Her older sister is a widow.</p>\n<p>Over the past couple of months, I've grown quite fond of the niece. We have emotionally bonded with each other. She clearly needs a father figure in her life and to be honest, I would not mind being that (it's all very healthy.) In all honesty, the two of us are fulfilling certain emotional needs in each other.</p>\n<p>The girl is very nice with me, but with her mother it's different.\nShe keeps on defying her to get a rise out of her (it's working). Here it is winter, and this weekend the child asked her mother if she could go swimming. Her mother told her no, because going swimming in the winter is a bad idea, and she decided to jump in the pool anyway.</p>\n<p>The mother started yelling at the child; she had a true meltdown. I have never seen a woman that angry before.</p>\n<p>I look at the child and see just how much pleasure the response she is getting gives her. I pulled her out of the swimming pool and told her to go to her room. After a few moments, the mom calmed down.</p>\n<p>You have already lost one parent and now you make your mother so angry that you risk losing another. If those school teachers of yours see one thing that leads them to suspect abuse in the house they are obligated by law to report it. You think the social worker will not take you away?</p>\n<p>She starts crying and I tell her I don't want to hear it. I then tell her that I do intend to eventually marry into this family and I won't allow her to ruin it for me.</p>\n<p>The thing is I'm a 35 year old bachelor that is having this parental role thrust on me. The mother should just have it go. If you give a child such control over your emotions, they will abuse it.</p>\n<p>Luckily I was present to de-escelate the situation but I won't always be there to do so. How do I convince her to not spank angrily?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","gender"],"owner":{"account_id":34040941,"reputation":19,"user_id":47246,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/84f07149783f3a6b4b02a9fd1eb03d48?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"SLP","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/47246/slp"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":1032,"closed_date":1722163578,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1722021526,"creation_date":1720846336,"question_id":44262,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44262/3-year-old-son-only-interested-in-girl-things","closed_reason":"Duplicate","title":"3 Year Old Son - Only Interested in “Girl” Things","body":"<p>My 2, almost 3, year old son is only interested in “girl” activities and play items. He has an older brother, who had no interest in this type of play so our house is mostly neutral or “boy” toys.</p>\n<p>My son loves jewelry, makeup, dresses, hair, princesses, the color pink etc.</p>\n<p>For now, I act interested in what he likes and I support him. No shaming in this home.</p>\n<p>This isn’t a post to ask what should do, or how to treat him. However, I’m curious, if anyone else has had a similar experience with their child at a young age and if their interests changed or if it stayed the same as an older child. I’ve read that this age is too young to come to any conclusions regarding gender or sexuality but I am curious on real life examples. If you’re comfortable sharing please do. Thank you.</p>\n"},{"tags":["education","age-appropriate"],"owner":{"account_id":6950583,"reputation":555,"user_id":35501,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Hivf3.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Kjara","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/35501/kjara"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":818,"answer_count":5,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1729358387,"creation_date":1720914057,"last_edit_date":1729358387,"question_id":44263,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44263/how-to-prepare-children-for-evil","title":"How to prepare children for evil","body":"<p>I want to prepare my children for life in the best possible way (as most parents do), and an important aspect of this is, in my opinion, teaching them about the evil things humans are capable of, so that they can look for signs and try to avoid or fight it. Maybe the classic teaching in this regard is &quot;don't take anything from strangers, don't go with them, no matter how nice they seem&quot;. But this does not tell the child what the problem really is. I believe the child is better prepared if they know what could happen if they followed the stranger. So the child should know about for example: rape, torture, drug addiction, violence because of differing sexual orientation or religion... to name a few. But they shouldn't be so terrified that they cannot function properly (like feeling the need to run away whenever a stranger talks to them).</p>\n<p>I do think they should confront all those horrors (by reading, watching films, talking about it) in order to be prepared, but I don't know when and in what dosage. I don't want to turn them into psychic wrecks that are too afraid to leave the house, after all.</p>\n<p>I am looking for guidelines on how to determine which kind of atrocities my child can mentally handle, and in what way (at their age or current developmental stage). Are there any parenting books on this? Or any other resources?</p>\n<p>For movies, there are age recommendations. And the people who give out these recommendations must have some basis for their decisions. What is that basis?</p>\n<p>To give a concrete example where I want to teach but don't know how: I have a 5 year old son. We recently listened to a happy-go-lucky children's podcast episode that was about love and affection and that two people of the same sex being affectionate towards each other is as OK as the standard male-female affection. And in the podcast it was said that &quot;some people do not know this yet, they think only male-female is OK, so we should tell them: love is for everyone!&quot;. As if homophobia was just missing knowledge like illiteracy... My take on this is: Sure, there are people - mainly kids - who think it is not OK because they have been told so, but not reflected it in any way. But there are also many people who have CHOSEN to find it not OK, and among them still enough to readily use violence against not only those who are homosexual but also against those who promote accepting homosexuality as OK. And I want my son to know this, so that he does not run around happily-go-luckily, being blind to the evil in humans, telling men who are busy throwing gay couples from roofs that they just haven't heard the news yet. Because he will be next.\n(Some might argue that the topic of homosexuality is not appropriate for a 5 year old and I tend to agree, but it is impossible - at least for me - to check every bit of media before I expose him to it (especially when I found a series to be suitable, I do not pre-watch/listen every episode), just like I cannot plan every step of his life. So at some point he will be confronted with something new, challenging, disturbing. That's why I am asking this question.)</p>\n"},{"tags":["primary-schooler","safety"],"owner":{"account_id":2342947,"reputation":251,"user_id":47956,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/R0Zkb.png?s=256","display_name":"Jonny","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/47956/jonny"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":7750,"answer_count":6,"score":15,"last_activity_date":1722590791,"creation_date":1722455636,"question_id":44273,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44273/childs-teddies-being-mean-after-dark","title":"Child&#39;s teddies &quot;being mean&quot; after dark","body":"<p>Our child (7f) has got bruises on her upper biceps. We asked about it and she has been sucking and biting on her arms in bed. She says that she needs comfort because some of her soft toys are being mean, e.g. saying things like &quot;I'm not listening to you&quot;.</p>\n<p>We have talked about it and it seems related to some other children in class saying similar things. We are on school holidays at the moment (England), so there doesn't seem to be any school related fix here atm. The soft toys can be divided into kind and unkind, and the unkind half have been removed from her bed.</p>\n<p>Is there anything else we should be doing? Is there anything deeper that we should be concerned about?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","development","speech","autism","hearing"],"owner":{"account_id":34482257,"reputation":21,"user_id":47961,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cb8d0d81597a4148a59150df2df90c06?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Jshoe ","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/47961/jshoe"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":754,"answer_count":3,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1723246179,"creation_date":1722478681,"last_edit_date":1722521747,"question_id":44276,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44276/2-y-o-with-speech-delay","title":"2 y/o with speech delay","body":"<p>My son turned 2 in April. He is currently in early intervention for speech, they also are working on his receptive language.</p>\n<p>When he was 10 months old he got ear tubes after having ear infections for several months. After his tubes were placed we felt like he stopped responding to us, when we called his name or yelled he wouldn’t look. We were concerned about his hearing for several months following the surgery and he couldn’t get through the first hearing test he had but was able to pass at his 2 year appointment. The ear doctor said that his ear infections and tubes had nothing to do with him not speaking but I truly felt that there was a clear difference after his surgery and when they did a pressure test on his ears at 16 months it seemed to correct the problem as he was way more responsive to us but still only saying minimal words.</p>\n<p>Well now we have been doing EI for almost 6 months, he has developed a few more words and signs and it’s more consistent with showing us what he wants. He loves to play with his sister and cousins and seeks out other kids when we go to play group or the park. Our EI therapist said she would like to see him be more reactive to her calling his name and for him to do more imitation play although he does it with us. He also gets hyper focused on a toy or activity he is playing and will ignore you if you try to get him to do a different task or activity which I feel is a typical for some toddlers but seems to concern our EI therapist . She has also suggested having him tested for autism but we feel he is too young and everyone who meets him who understands autism or has worked with kids with autism feel that he doesn’t show the typical signs.  I should add that he makes eye contact, interacts with other kids and adults that he knows well or even has only met a few times, he also is entering the two year old tantrum stage but it never lasts over 5 minutes if that and he is redirectable.</p>\n<p>I am curious peoples thoughts on waiting for testing or giving him more time to develop. He is also starting daycare in a few weeks which will be two full days every week. I just feel there is so much pressure to label kids and it’s becoming very overwhelming for my husband and myself as we do all the suggestions given to us and have seen progress but wish he was saying more. Thank you for any suggestions or words of encouragement.</p>\n"},{"tags":["pre-schooler","discipline"],"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":2918,"accepted_answer_id":44288,"answer_count":2,"score":11,"last_activity_date":1723073737,"creation_date":1722996246,"question_id":44287,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44287/four-year-old-pushing-and-positive-discipline-techniques","title":"Four Year Old Pushing and Positive Discipline Techniques","body":"<p>This isn't a major problem for us, but I'm curious to get a few perspectives on this issue. My wife and I have two boys, two and four, both close to two and four, and for the most part we try to use positive discipline techniques to correct their behavior. So far we don't really punish them, although we'll very occasionally raise our voice in a targeted way (usually only for the four year old at this point). We try to teach and mentor them into better behavior, rather than making them feel bad about things they've done.</p>\n<p>I think, for the most part, this has been successful and we've seen real improvements in self regulation in the four year old. He's very independent, and I would call him mature for his age. The two year old is too young to see much difference.</p>\n<p>However, occasionally the four year old will do something a little over the line like push younger brother completely unprompted, and in his last months at daycare he's really starting to test the educators. This is where the question comes in. A part of me thinks: this is all age appropriate for the most part, and he'll gradually grow out of it in the next few years. But he is becoming a little more brazen, and you can tell that he's becoming comfortable testing boundaries because there are no real repercussions to his behavior. For the most part he's well behaved, but when he wants to break a rule he'll break a rule.</p>\n<p>So then that leads to the question: do we introduce some kind of mild deterrent to encourage him to stop doing things like pushing his brother, or other ridiculous / dangerous stuff. Something like no TV for the night. Would that kind of thing be age appropriate, or does his behavior come down to not having the ability to fully self regulate?</p>\n"},{"tags":["autism"],"owner":{"account_id":34713156,"reputation":19,"user_id":48324,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/30327e9b5e25827d6f9b1ee476f91b51?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Randi O&#39;Dowd","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/48324/randi-odowd"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":227,"closed_date":1723821419,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1723659028,"creation_date":1723256156,"question_id":44292,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44292/1-5-year-old-boy-doesnt-seem-fully-connected-socially-compared-to-his-social","closed_reason":"Not suitable for this site","title":"1.5 year old boy, doesn&#39;t seem fully connected socially, compared to his social butterfly outgoing twin brother","body":"<p>I have been worried that my 1.5 year old son is possibly on the autism spectrum. He is. twin so unfortunately I do compare the two. However, it is usually a one way compare, why is he not acting or doing what his brother is?</p>\n<p>My son is very laid back, he does have bursts of energy like any kid but most of the time seems in a great mood, happy and content with anything going on.  He is verbal and can talk in nearly full sentences, his short sentences are great and spoken correctly although sometimes long sentences/conversations he tries to have could be labeled as gibberish, but it does make sense and is almost always appropriate to the situation. If that makes sense? He can essentially ask for example ( He wonders about his papa tractor and its horn and likes to tell me that its at his papas house) He will say something like &quot; Papas beep beep (tractor) gibberish gibberish at home, papa at home? beep beep. &quot; He will tell you this with great concern for where his papas tractor is at. He when he asks questions gibberish added or not he will have it backed with the correct emotion. &quot;where's sissy?&quot; he will hold his hands out and look at you for an answer. He is hard to make consistent eye contact with, he will some of the time but it is not held long. He has no problem continuing the interaction without eye contact. This has been the ultimate RED FLAG for me, this is what initiated our visit to the peds. He is being seen my Early On, but I they are not assessing him other than once a month visit where they come play with him.</p>\n<p>He knows so many words, he knows how most things work. He can work the TV, when the next episode needs to be selected. He doesn't seem to have any sensory issues, if anyone has issues with sensory it's his twin brother, that boy cant stand pants or long sleeve shirts.</p>\n<p>There's just something about him that seems off to me, tonight we went to the fair. His brother was so excited for the animals, saying bye bye to them by name, saying hi. Telling his &quot;Nana horse&quot; where his brother in question had no interest in interacting with the animals. He doesn't say hi to strangers like his sister and brother do. Tonight he took a lot of coaxing to get involved and engaged and it honestly broke my heart to see him just not care interact with us, unless we were trying extremely hard, like having to tickle him to get him to become involved. He honestly just loves to walk and wanted nothing but to walk and run around which made him upset multiple times.</p>\n<p>He does play with his brother and sister, they parallel play and play together. He will present to sleep and snore, he had a stuff animal cat the other day and was pretending it was running alone the couch and floor meowing the entire time. He will hug the stuff animals and mimic the things they should do. He will copy our dogs and things we say. He will interact with Mickey Mouse clubhouse, sing the theme song and look to you at certain parts because those are his fav. He will follow directions and complete tasks with effort on our part but what 1.5 year old doesn't need help completing tasks asked of them like throwing their dirty diapers away in kitchen garbage that is located in a cabinet that they have to open.</p>\n<p>I just cant let it do, he isn't as animated as his siblings. Hes just different from them. I totally know no two children are the same but has anyone else had a child that just constantly teetered on being autistic or not?</p>\n"},{"tags":["divorce"],"owner":{"account_id":34875886,"reputation":1,"user_id":48527,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fe62fb2ad7053e04d4f68ea7361f6461?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Hazelnut ","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/48527/hazelnut"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":68,"closed_date":1723779971,"answer_count":0,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1723735618,"creation_date":1723735618,"question_id":44297,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44297/my-parents-are-getting-a-divorce-how-do-i-prevent-it-if-i-am-the-cause","closed_reason":"Duplicate","title":"My parents are getting a divorce how do I prevent it if I am the cause?","body":"<p>So I play a sport and I am a state player\nfor the past four years, I have always been on and off badminton while going through thick and thin with my sisters and family . Every time I do something wrong, my whole family gets scolded And I have to live with that thought that I’m the cause of everyone’s misery . My mother (especially)gets scolded for whether it’s not giving me proper nutrition or not enough sleep .my dad is very very ambitious and is very supportive but he gets worked up and hits people when things aren’t his way . My mom just absorbs the pain and takes all the negativity in .. My dad really wants me to be an Olympic Champion. I’m not saying I don’t want to be a champion.. I have the body the height and the money and support from my family … I am also trying my best . recently My dad hired a personal caretaker to take care of me to manage my badminton schedule and train me.. but recently the caretaker brought his family over from another state which angers my dad since he is putting less effort and less time so that he can spend more time with his family . I understand since my dad is paying him 500 per day for a month and he is spending around 3 hours a day only and his cost monthly is around 15,000 ( people in my country earn a average 3000-5000per month ) . And my dad thinks he is doing it for the money and it is good money but I can assure you that it is not for the money . Since he is pretty well off and has 4 successful sons . So yea , today my dad got angry again since he probably didn’t sleep well and had scolded my mom and me because I didn’t take a nap in the afternoon .. everytime he tells me to quit but I write him a letter and we just continue again it has been going on this cycle for forever and he is losing hope in me . I have started to change my attitude now but since he is spending thousands per month he expects even more so I don’t know what I should do . Also, I don’t want or quit badminton I do it because I don’t want his and my effort , his money , my sacrifices and everyones time to go to waste .\nanyways now my dad wants to divorce my mom because she doesn’t take proper care of me .. which makes me the cause … what can I do to stop it ? I don’t want to live with this burden that I have to carry “ my parents divorced because of me “ how do I stop it ??</p>\n"},{"tags":["co-parenting","custody"],"owner":{"account_id":34908642,"reputation":21,"user_id":48576,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/4bc01f4b47ce8baaea02929825b5821e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Pedro","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/48576/pedro"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":417,"closed_date":1724676836,"answer_count":1,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1723905210,"creation_date":1723880073,"question_id":44299,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44299/my-daughter-is-12-years-old-and-she-doesnt-want-to-live-with-her-mother-anymore","closed_reason":"Not suitable for this site","title":"My daughter is 12 years old and she doesn&#39;t want to live with her mother anymore","body":"<p>There is no court order and I don't pay child support. There is no abuse going on that I know of. My daughter simply wants to stay in Texas living with me. I have no animosity or bad feelings towards my daughter's mother. We actually get a long quite well. I want to respect my daughters decision. I don't want to fight with her mother. What can her mother legally do if I don't send my daughter back to her?</p>\n"},{"tags":["education","communication","emotional-well-being","emotional-development"],"owner":{"account_id":13325563,"reputation":141,"user_id":48651,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/c4657c931c999ce06e83fa3d06808f0f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"antonro","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/48651/antonro"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":257,"answer_count":1,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1724117648,"creation_date":1724059707,"question_id":44302,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44302/in-positive-education-what-phrase-follows-the-emotional-validation","title":"In positive education, what phrase follows the emotional validation?","body":"<p>I’m watching a course on positive parenting and secure attachment, where the instructor emphasizes the importance of &quot;validating the child’s emotions.&quot; As an example, they mention a situation where the child wants to have ice cream for breakfast, and after denying it, our job as parents &quot;is not to avoid their frustration, but to acknowledge it.&quot; So, what we need to do is maintain boundaries and then empathize by saying things like &quot;I know you were really looking forward to that ice cream&quot; or &quot;You’re feeling a bit disappointed.&quot;</p>\n<p>When I was a child, the conversation would have been &quot;You can’t have ice cream for breakfast,&quot; followed by &quot;because I said so&quot; or &quot;just because.&quot; Then, the older family members from my generation, like cousins who now have kids aged 8-10, would say &quot;You can’t have ice cream for breakfast,&quot; followed by a lengthy attempt to reason with the child, &quot;because it’s not healthy,&quot; &quot;because you already have your cereal,&quot; etc.</p>\n<p>My question is, exactly following the positive parenting methodology and the approach recommended by the course instructor, how does the conversation continue after the phrases that validate emotions? What exactly follows after &quot;I know you were really looking forward to that ice cream&quot;?</p>\n"},{"tags":["development","cognitive-development","attention","focus","listening"],"owner":{"account_id":5790163,"reputation":9,"user_id":35552,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6360e49969b73cce596c99d9a26e7c40?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user1622","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/35552/user1622"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":473,"answer_count":1,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1784905650,"creation_date":1724243773,"question_id":44304,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44304/seeming-contradiction-with-respect-to-selective-attention-in-preschoolers","title":"Seeming contradiction with respect to selective attention in preschoolers","body":"<p>I can't seem to make sense of a seeming contradiction between scientific research and my observation:</p>\n<p>When my preschooler is watching a beetle or my 7-year old is playing Nintendo Switch and I say something, anything, really, the response is zero. I am invisible / non-existent.</p>\n<p>Research on the other hand claims that kids have short attention spans and generally don't do selective attention but take in all information.</p>\n<p>So why is it that I can't break my kids' focus? Why do they become so focussed and engrossed that they don't hear me even though they are supposed to have a short attention span and bad focussing skills?</p>\n<p>Is there any research on this and if so, could someone please point me to it?</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","primary-schooler","emotional-development"],"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":1073,"answer_count":1,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1724702670,"creation_date":1724397673,"question_id":44306,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44306/how-to-handle-excessive-self-pity","title":"How to handle excessive self pity","body":"<p>Sometimes my 11 year-old child will fall to excessive self pity. Two examples:</p>\n<p>He was very angry about something and tossed away his phone, a corner of the phone cracked and he thought he had broken it completely and permanently. I heard him wailing from the street 200 meters away. I thought something terrible had happed, like maybe he fell and seriously injured himself. His frustration is perfectly understandable: he was already frustrated and then he lost his temper and thought he broke his favourite and most expensive play-thing. But he actively fuels the fire, the crying goes on when all the tears are spent, in a theatrical manner. He throws himself on the couch, grinds his teeth, balls his fists.</p>\n<p>Another example: The teacher gaves us exercises to practice writing and spelling at home to help speed up his writing. He hates doing this because its school-work outside school and unlike regular homework this is not something the whole class has to do. He procastrinates, laments how unfair it is he has to do this while his friends don't, then claims he cannot spell and will do everything wrong anyway. He then cries, holds the pencil limply in his hand. While focussing on making a theater and arguing with parents he will make silly mistakes, like not finishing a word or forgetting letters, this fuels more crying and 'see I can't do this'. Last time he was hitting himself in the head with both fists after spending almost 1 hour on writing down two sentences and 5 words.</p>\n<p>Again, this is a frustrating situation, but he is making it so much worse. The task is intended to last 15 minutes.\nI understand I should avoid these situations and help him deal with frustrations but sometimes frustrating things happen. I have read 'How to talk to kids' and I try not to tell him off for his feelings, but I do want him to stop fanning the flames of negative emotions.</p>\n"},{"tags":["development","safety","education","school","age-appropriate"],"owner":{"account_id":11942273,"reputation":347,"user_id":36024,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://graph.facebook.com/1324865164215307/picture?type=large","display_name":"George Ntoulos","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36024/george-ntoulos"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":2035,"protected_date":1727787747,"closed_date":1724990342,"accepted_answer_id":44309,"answer_count":2,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1727787737,"creation_date":1724865613,"last_edit_date":1727787737,"question_id":44308,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44308/walk-or-drive-to-school","closed_reason":"Opinion-based","title":"Walk or Drive to school?","body":"<p>In Greece I see many (not as many as I see in Japanese movies) elementary school kids (6-12) walking on their own to their school. I myself almost always got drived to school (my parents never walked me to school). I only regularly walked myself to school in highschool (I was 14 because I was born in December). I am interested and curious about parental choices (maybe my parents could have done better, I am close to parenting age, seeing young children on the road leaves me wondering).</p>\n<p>What should you do with your kids when the distance to school is less than a mile or two? Assume a relatively safe area when it comes to crimes, a slight uphill slope ~100 metres, and some navigation problems and risks of traffic accidents. How does this choice change as they grow older (probably they gain navigation skills, independence, can better manage their wake-sleep cycles)?</p>\n<p>The options I see are three:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Walk them there</li>\n<li>Send them off on their own</li>\n<li>Drive them to school</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Walking has the benefit of freedom of motion. (Driving a mile or two with heavy traffic is not the best thing and is unpredictable; half an hour on foot is usually half an hour while a 5-minute drive can become 15-20.) If they walk on their own they gain independence and might socialize on their way there. (It also saves you time to leave for work in the morning but the main focus is parenting.) If their parents walk them there that is safer (especially at younger ages with traffic accidents) and that is some quality time spent with the kids.</p>\n<p>Driving can save some minutes of (their) sleep especially in the morning (unless there is a lot of traffic or you can't find a place to park).</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler"],"owner":{"account_id":35375998,"reputation":21,"user_id":49152,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/V0a1f0Kt.jpg?s=256","display_name":"MsPNR","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/49152/mspnr"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":108,"answer_count":0,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1725286867,"creation_date":1725286673,"last_edit_date":1725286867,"question_id":44312,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44312/eating-habit-of-a-16-month-old-toddler","title":"Eating habit of a 16 month old toddler","body":"<p>I'm writing here to know if something I'm doing wrong. I have 16 month old toddler who never seems interested in breakfast since 7-8 months.<br />\nSometimes he eats when distracted only whether he drinks morning milk before breakfast or not. He is always fussy in the morning but yes he likes anything to drink so I started giving him smoothy types like Rice or oats powder mixed with dry fruits powder + 1 soaked date with fruit like banana/avocado/pear+ frozen strawberry blended with whole milk available here in riyadh.</p>\n<p>Can someone guide me is it ok, should I consider this breakfast or snack? Am I making him habitual of smoothie? Actually I have tried everything like no milk before breakfast, our same adult breakfast, avocado, french toast, pancake etc but he seems never interested.</p>\n<p>Our pediatrician suggested him formula till 2 years because till now he has 2 lower teeth so the pediatrician has given reason of vitamin which is not in fresh milk so I give formula only 2 times and whole milk smoothie in breakfast. He seems more interested in fruits, probiotic and dairy whole day rather than grain or cereals.</p>\n<p>Please suggest.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","bedtime"],"owner":{"account_id":35405079,"reputation":21,"user_id":49199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/bbfcaa8ea6e8ff0e601d1ca5dbfd0079?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Nick Homewood","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/49199/nick-homewood"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":359,"answer_count":3,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1728551053,"creation_date":1725393043,"question_id":44315,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44315/help-for-getting-toddler-to-sleep","title":"Help for getting toddler to sleep","body":"<p>Our daughter who is 2 and in her own room now has started to play up when going to bed, we bath her, have relaxed environment, tell a story and she is tired but when I cuddle her now she has started rolling around and getting up, playing up. I would like to leave her but she cries and my wife doesn’t want to leave her so stuck a little bit as it’s tough, but I feel she is testing us but she is definitely tired,wondered if there was anything we can do? Thankyou</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","middle-childhood"],"owner":{"account_id":1665264,"reputation":2243,"user_id":7778,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":33,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1edb320793125bf1d36ed27b87b9c51?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"localhost","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7778/localhost"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":177,"closed_date":1725980986,"answer_count":1,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1739635114,"creation_date":1725745103,"last_edit_date":1739635114,"question_id":44323,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44323/how-to-create-a-routinue-to-almost-9-yo","closed_reason":"Duplicate","title":"How to create a routinue to almost 9 yo","body":"<p>My kid is going to be 9 yo in 2 months, Lately I had cut cable tv to cut his tv and got him bicycle, he plays well outside but he is having hard time understanding how to set boundries. We talked about how to do study, games and then outdoor but if we don't stop him, it is playing all day or tv. he has 0 interest in studying or gaining knowledge, I am very worried.</p>\n<p>Now I have noticed that he is skipping brushing or washing face, we tried to see if he notices and do it himself, but either he skip with a guilt look or lie.</p>\n<p>We are worried and I do not know what to do?</p>\n<p>Edit: Now that I tired some things told to me in answer like making a routinue, giving him 20 minutes do so something and asking him to do everyday e.g.</p>\n<p>write down hard words from his story book and write it down,when done find the word in dictionary. It been a month since he did his last assignment but when today I asked to show me and nothing done since last month, 28 days ago.</p>\n<p>Typical day for him look like, waking up and start watching tv until someone is going out then go with him, do a movie marathon or cartoon marathon.</p>\n<p>This is very worrysome for me as I fear in the digital age he and his little brother is loosing their ability to focus (I strictly told them no YT shorts, yet they do watch it) as I have short focus as well and struggle a lot.</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","siblings"],"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":426,"accepted_answer_id":44329,"answer_count":3,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1726303260,"creation_date":1725847019,"question_id":44324,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44324/two-boys-how-and-when-will-their-relationship-change","title":"Two boys: how and when will their relationship change?","body":"<p>I have two boys, one is 4y4m, the other is 2y5m. At this time they're finally starting to play pretty well and more cooperatively with each other. Although we do still need to be around because hitting still happens and tempers flare.</p>\n<p>What I'm curious to know is how their relationship with each other is likely to evolve in the next number of years. At what age are we going to be able to step away from them more fully and just let them play? And what will happen after that stage? Basically just wanting to get an idea of how and when it's likely to evolve from the standpoint of how we'll need to parent them.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","sleep","safety","habits"],"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":595,"answer_count":2,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1727046861,"creation_date":1726690883,"question_id":44335,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44335/3-year-old-wants-to-sleep-in-closet","title":"3 Year Old Wants To Sleep In Closet","body":"<p>Our 3 year old has a preferred sleeping place. It is not the comfy mattress we have set up for him - it is his bedroom closet with the sliding doors.</p>\n<p>If we put him to bed where we want him to sleep at night, every time we go to get him in the morning he will be in his closet. He will even drag a blanket into the closet and throw himself down on it instead of using his bed.</p>\n<p>I suspect it might be because his closet is darker than the rest of his room, or part of a habit he has developed all on his own.</p>\n<p>First of all, <em>is</em> this a problem if he is comfortable and safe in there, and if it is how do we stop his closet-sleeping?</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","teen","motivation","respect","responsible"],"owner":{"account_id":953474,"reputation":277,"user_id":21356,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f8fb41d5bd28c2c93dd83ba7f6c7393c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user979189","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21356/user979189"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":448,"answer_count":1,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1730811525,"creation_date":1727195702,"last_edit_date":1727208135,"question_id":44338,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44338/how-to-help-13-year-old-to-be-more-open-motivated-and-responsible","title":"How to help 13 year old to be more open, motivated and responsible","body":"<p>I have a son - 13 yrs old. He is doing well in his school gifted in both Math and Language arts.\nBut he has a few behavioral / personality issues that seem to be bothering us especially now since he is reaching his puberty age and looking for more independence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>He is not particularly strong willed. He tried a few things and giving up as soon as he think it is too hard for him. For ex. I enrolled him in karate class at the age of 6, he reached very close to black belt but now left it since he is finding it too hard.</li>\n<li>He is too picky in eating, small for his age and he seems to have accepted it that he is different from his peers but at the same time he also feels inferior that he is small and not as strongly built as his peers.</li>\n<li>He is not physically too active, he likes to lie around play games or watch vidoes from his favorite youtube channel and gets less involved in physical activities. To motivate him, we got a table tennis table, pickle ball set, swimming twice a week, tennis classes, badminton etc. And I encourage him to come join with me and his younger brother playing these games and other board games and family activities but none of these interests him.</li>\n<li>Till now he used to read a lot but now he also lost interest in reading.</li>\n<li>The biggest issue now I am facing is he is not motivated to do anything. He has in front of him his 2 yr younger brother who is so much into trying all sorts of things, trying all kinds of technical things including building his own gaming PC, building custom RC cars, trying all kinds of other STEM experiments his age. I am mentioning his younger brother not to compare both of them but just that his yonger brothers example in front of him doesnt work to motivate him. However whatever little he helped his yonger brother do his stuff, he is quick to take credit for what he did.</li>\n<li>He doesnt have friends his age, he talks and likes to hang around with his younger brothers friends. This might be linked to the point 2 above.</li>\n<li>He has strong attitude problem which is why he is not a type someone would like to be aroud with. Very quick to blame others for something wrong that happened with him, not willing to take responsibility for his own choices. Likes to argue when you try to talk to him about what he needs to work on or improve upon. Recently his tennis coach complained that he tried to talk to him about what he needs to improve upon, but instead of working on his mistake he argued about what his coach did wrong.</li>\n<li>Clumsy. I have to keep reminding him about tying his shoe laces, brushing his teeth, combing his hairs, cleaning his room etc. He is very forgetful and keeps missing his basic chores.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Things I do as his dad -</p>\n<ol>\n<li>I spend a lot of time with him take him to swimming, tennis, frisby, badminton etc. Also play family board games, chess and ping-pong.</li>\n<li>I read a lot, about 2-3 hours a day as a hobby, and I think it may motivate him to restart his reading.</li>\n<li>I talk a lot about making healthier eating choices, making better overall/holistic life-style choices.</li>\n<li>We always have meals together and I initiate discussion about how he is doing at school, whether he needs any help at anything and a bunch of other family fun/silly talks.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Inspite of all negative points I mentioned in the previous section, one thing never to forget is he is very gentle, sweet and innocent child when all is going well. Very willing to help others with what he is good at.\nAnd I as his dad can relate to all those things mentioned above since I was and still am pretty much the same personality. I hit my maturity age pretty late at around 20-22 yrs and I know no one liked to be around me in my childhood, I learnt my lessons a hard/ugly and painful way and tried to improve upon a bunch of those things which can make me hard to be with.\nI know he is just following me, but I just want to see if there is any better way he can learn his lessons, get motivated, be more open to try new things.\nI could be doing certain things wrong which is triggering a few of these issues and looking for some suggestions about what I can do to help him grow well.</p>\n<p>Please help!</p>\n"},{"tags":["primary-schooler","education"],"owner":{"account_id":1665264,"reputation":2243,"user_id":7778,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":33,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1edb320793125bf1d36ed27b87b9c51?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"localhost","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7778/localhost"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":392,"answer_count":2,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1729268190,"creation_date":1727454892,"question_id":44341,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44341/my-son-disturbed-after-no-one-shared-a-snack-how-do-i-teach-him-lesson-from-thi","title":"My son disturbed after no one shared a snack. How do I teach him lesson from this?","body":"<p>My son is 8yo, today at school everyone was exchanging sweets, he didn't mention it to us so we never knew it was a day of exchanging sweets. He felt heartbroken as no one is the class shared with him. Is it concerning and damaging as well. I told him that</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>we cannot expect people to share, if you share that doesn't mean someone else is having same values. Mom, dad and siblings are best friends as He also complain about not having friends.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He started his schooling in private school, due to finance, I took him out after year 2 but the mindset of private vs pupuil is huge! But did I tell my son right thing?</p>\n<p>My second one is very jolly and he started in public school, today he felt down and he tells me someone call him dumb in class, I would talk to teacher but feel like they are not settling in or we as a parent are feeling too much?</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler"],"owner":{"account_id":36027439,"reputation":41,"user_id":50147,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/3fc051fbf0b5e90d3a441e523d10cc31?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Jaimee Magee","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/50147/jaimee-magee"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":495,"answer_count":2,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1728563836,"creation_date":1727572081,"last_edit_date":1727798357,"question_id":44342,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44342/how-to-navigate-a-2-year-old-saying-stop-it-to-everyone","title":"How to navigate a 2 year old saying &quot;stop it&quot; to everyone","body":"<p>My almost 2 year old has just entered the &quot;mine&quot; stage and is having a hard time sharing toys and taking turns.  I understand this is developmentally appropriate for her age and explain turn taking and such to her each time we hear &quot;No, mine.&quot;</p>\n<p>Recently however I told her when she wants daddy to stop tickling her she should say &quot;Stop&quot; or &quot;Stop it&quot; instead of the usual &quot;No!&quot;</p>\n<p>This worked well for the games but now she will tell anyone to &quot;Stop it!&quot; When they walk into a room, if they're on a toy she wanted to use, literally anything and all day long.</p>\n<p>How should I approach this?</p>\n<p>I have been redirecting her when I can, trying to provide more words for what she's saying.</p>\n<p>I will say things like &quot;No, she is doing cooking right now and can't stop. What can you see her cooking?&quot; Or &quot;They are using the slide right now and you would like a turn? Let's wait here for our turn.&quot;</p>\n<p>Does anyone have any advice?</p>\n<p>I've had some family member complain that it's rude and while it does feel that way I know it is just her learning how to use those words and in what context.  Expanding on her sense of self. Experimenting with what she can control.</p>\n<p>I don't want to make it worse or solidify her use of the words but I also want her to know she can tell someone to stop touching her for example</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","parents","emotional-well-being","working-parents"],"owner":{"account_id":24651725,"reputation":291,"user_id":50279,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJzq_kPT1gEQsSUwhCTHySuL00iL8JmvyuSBOpgr=k-s256","display_name":"shaqiya laura","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/50279/shaqiya-laura"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":5647,"answer_count":5,"score":19,"last_activity_date":1728069124,"creation_date":1727788379,"last_edit_date":1727878914,"question_id":44345,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44345/how-do-i-handle-guilt-after-yelling-at-my-child","title":"How do I handle guilt after yelling at my child?","body":"<p>I'm a father of two, and my eldest child is 4 years and 2 months old. I work from home while caring for my first child, and today I feel like I overreacted and yelled at them.</p>\n<p>This has been building up over the past week because my child has been very difficult when it comes to eating, and I've been feeling frustrated. Additionally, I feel some anger toward my wife's family, as my child tends to get sick every time I pick them up to stay with me.</p>\n<p>Two weeks ago, my child was hospitalized for a urinary tract infection (UTI), and this week they've been complaining of pain and having fevers every night. I'm confused and overwhelmed with guilt over my actions today.</p>\n<p>What should I do to address this guilt and manage my emotions better? I'm really struggling with feelings of confusion and regret.</p>\n"},{"tags":["sleep","night-time"],"owner":{"account_id":103499,"reputation":1,"user_id":50331,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/870d0def7cb277ed3218bbd86ec1ce52?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Blazor","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/50331/blazor"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":222,"answer_count":1,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1729268703,"creation_date":1727862449,"last_edit_date":1727875325,"question_id":44347,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44347/8-to-9-hours-night-time-sleep-but-extremely-cranky-in-the-morning-13mo","title":"8 to 9 hours night time sleep but extremely cranky in the morning (13mo)","body":"<p>My 13 months old son is not a good sleeper. He wakes up 1-2 times on average during the night and stays in his crib for a maximum of 10 hours, totaling, at best, 9 hours of sleep overall.</p>\n<p>He usually wakes up between 6 and 7 AM. We take him to daycare between 8:30 and 9 AM, but almost always, by that time, he already starts showing signs of sleepiness (rubbing his eyes, yawning, and becoming irritable and whiny).</p>\n<p>I think he doesn't get enough rest at night, but after trying various adjustments (like going to bed earlier, later, closer to or further from dinner), I can't get him to accept being taken back to sleep if he has already slept at least 9 hours.</p>\n<p>What do you think the problem is?</p>\n"},{"tags":["attention","attachment","foster-parenting"],"owner":{"account_id":2188762,"reputation":109,"user_id":50507,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cde127add1f164678fd6c0e9eccdda10?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Le Frite","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/50507/le-frite"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":465,"answer_count":3,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1751537104,"creation_date":1728077098,"last_edit_date":1751537104,"question_id":44355,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44355/is-time-really-the-only-solution-for-a-child-with-attachment-issues","title":"Is time really the only solution for a child with attachment issues?","body":"<p>My partner and I have a 3 year old biological son and a foster 3 year old girl that's been full-time with us for 10 months. I'll gloss over the details, but I have never developed any attachment nor have I felt love for the little girl. I have still acted as a dad for her as best as I could, but it has drained me and I've had a burnout. As a result I told my partner that I would stop being responsible for her, and minimize my time around her, until I figure a way to not be so unhappy in her presence. My partner lives with her mother and I live 5 mins away, we've always been both quite independent of each other - so this arrangement is something that works for us currently.</p>\n<p>Obviously things could be better. I see my partner and my son less as a result of this arrangement. If I can manage to spend time with this little girl without being mentally exhausted, it's all the better for everyone. I see three ways this could happen:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>I somehow become able to tolerate the behavioral issues of this little girl</li>\n<li>We find a way to discipline her so she behaves</li>\n<li>She begins feeling securely attached</li>\n</ol>\n<p>On (1): I don't have any idea of how to proceed. I am able to stay cool in the face of the occasional tantrums from my son. But when it comes from her, I am way more susceptible. She is constantly demanding for attention in whatever way possible and it drives me crazy. Without feeling attached to her, I don't see how I could withstand that on a regular basis. Even if I were to work on some anger management, it wouldn't help with the mental exhaustion. I have been thinking of therapy but I don't see exactly which kind, considering that my reaction is fairly normal.</p>\n<p>On (2): I am biased but I think that the way I do it works. I am caring but strict. I will give her affection during bonding time, and never when she asks for it in an excessive or inappropriate manner. After a couple of months together, she has come to behave like a regular child when I am alone with her, and I can tolerate her then. But it only works if it's just the two of us. My partner is not as strict, as her attachment to her is different and as she does not want to feel cruel in difficult times. This little girl knows that and she will regularly push my partner to the extreme so she can get attention. When we're looking from professional advice, we essentially get told to bear with it. That we should tell her that her behavior is inappropriate and that she matters to us without needing her to draw our attention to us. But isolating her as punishment is supposedly a no-no, and that's the only thing I can do not to go insane. We've been following these guidelines for weeks, and I don't see any change happening in her.</p>\n<p>On (3): I have tried many things. I read a book on attachment issues. We've been to parent-child bonding activities together. We've seen quite a few professionals. Still, this girl is only preoccupied by whether her foster mom or dad are paying attention to her. We've been told that 10 months living with us is nothing, that it'll take many more years to <em>maybe</em> get there. I am in denial about that. I refuse to believe that there isn't a way a child who has been separated from her parents can chill out with her new family after a couple of months. How ridiculous is the human condition that a child with attachment issues will do precisely the things that put them at risk of not being kept in their foster family? I get that she's testing us but to what point? It's not helping her situation.</p>\n<p>A couple of notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>My partner sees this child as her own, and has no plans of sending her away ever.</li>\n<li>I make no bets in what the future will be. I could get attached to this girl. I could also not be attached, but become able to tolerate her. The current arrangement I have with my partner could last also a long time.</li>\n<li>There are currently no tensions with the way things are. All four of us are doing ok. Given our way of life, there would be nothing to gain in a proper separation with my partner.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p>July 2025 edit: Thanks to everyone who replied, I have carefully read your answers and truly appreciate them. I want to add some context:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The situation has evolved so that we spend more time together as 4, but it remains that I am not feeling serene, connected, attached to our girl. My &quot;attachment&quot; to her is unfortunately conditional: when she acts like a normal little girl I am happy to have her around, but when all she does is seek out our attention, I just wish she'd be gone. I do my best to treat her equally to my son, with whom I have such an easy time feeling compassion for.</li>\n<li>I have autism. A lot of you picked up on the fact that I speak rather coldly and bluntly about the situation, and you've hit home. I have this very emotionally detached manner of speaking about my life, but the good news is that it paints a darker image than what actually is. It's difficult to reason about my attachment with our girl and to put myself in her shoes. My autism is definitely a large part of the equation, and it's hard to figure out whether it will be at all possible for me to bond with her, even following the good advice I have been given here and in therapy. But I can't just rule it out on that basis.</li>\n<li>Becoming a foster parent was initially something I wanted to &quot;try&quot;, and despite being warned early on that there was no such thing as trying a child, I played along with my partner who was deeply willing to be a foster parent. It was the second day I visited our little girl that I realized it would be very difficult for me, and my partner was not at all ready to turn back then. We had several arguments and the decision was very difficult to make. I have considered leaving my partner months later, but we love eachother very much, so I stuck around best as I could.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"tags":["sleep-training"],"owner":{"account_id":36296836,"reputation":31,"user_id":50648,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/15861b1f2f9feae890b35dd79d7b4854?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user50648","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/50648/user50648"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":288,"answer_count":0,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1728285373,"creation_date":1728285373,"question_id":44360,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44360/why-is-my-15-months-old-waking-in-the-night-screaming-and-staying-awake-for-1-5","title":"Why is my 15 months old waking in the night screaming and staying awake for 1.5 hours","body":"<p>Our 15 month old has never been a great sleeper but 2 months ago she started waking in the night screaming - sounds like in pain or scared. She cannot be easily calmed down but when she is she tries to get back to sleep but she fidgets and moves so much it takes about 1.5 hours.\nTo cope we have started sleeping next to her but we know this isn’t a great idea. We want to get back to sleep training again but are worried about what is causing it and what is best to do.\nHas anyone experienced this? Worked out what it is? Ideas of what to do?\nThank you!</p>\n"},{"tags":["social-development","video-games","science"],"owner":{"account_id":1512052,"reputation":863,"user_id":38595,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2147c68b7ec5d32424a717c801525d56?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Travis Well","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38595/travis-well"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":809,"answer_count":3,"score":8,"last_activity_date":1749112532,"creation_date":1729140173,"last_edit_date":1729208827,"question_id":44368,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44368/what-is-the-science-on-preteen-kids-playing-moderate-less-than-9-h-wk-video-g","title":"What is the science on preteen kids playing moderate (less than 9 h /wk) video games?","body":"<p>What are long term effects of preteen kids playing moderate video games? Or is it the same as any screen time?\nThe axes I'm most interested in are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>competitive vs cooperative,</li>\n<li>screen time vs video game time (passive vs active),</li>\n<li>playing together in-person vs playing alone (regardless of online multiplayer)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I'm clicking through search results on pubmed but I'm not so good at this. I am only interested in projected long term psychosocial effects.</p>\n<p>The backstory is that I have a couple kids who are currently not playing games much. I have started playing games with them for cuddle time because when I start to play they come and cuddle with me which is, frankly, good for my mental health. So it's daddy time. Social, not a babysitter, and nonviolent. But it is addictive. They ask me to play the next day, or again the same evening. Honestly I've taken it as an opportunity to teach moderation, and emphasize that we have to be able to stop quickly for any reason. Like a conversation, homework, bedtime, etc. I have repeated (and modeled) this lesson many times. But now my partner is concerned because she sees the signs of addiction too. So what do longitudinal studies on video games say?</p>\n<p>There is no chance we aren't going to moderate the time, and while I plan to allow occasional solo play (eg 3D mental rotation is something my daughter can benefit from practicing and I have a couple puzzle games that are good for that), mainly I want to use games to get cuddles while I still can.</p>\n<p>One significant factor is that my younger son is autistic and we are therefore appropriately concerned about his long term social development, which is a challenge currently.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","food"],"owner":{"account_id":7895782,"reputation":221,"user_id":51415,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f8c02e1a484f62e2d24a1a44328cf385?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"SamOrd","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/51415/samord"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":3641,"answer_count":5,"score":12,"last_activity_date":1730473293,"creation_date":1729599686,"question_id":44375,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44375/how-can-i-encourage-my-toddler-to-try-new-foods","title":"How can I encourage my toddler to try new foods?","body":"<p>I have a two year old daughter who is generally a very good eater. When she was a bit younger she was always willing to at least take a bite of two of any new food that I would put on her plate, but lately she has started to completely refuse anything outside of her set of favorite foods. Those favorite foods are pretty varied, so it could be worse, but I really want to encourage her to try more things (especially vegetables, where she unsurprisingly the most picky).</p>\n<p>I've tried including a food that I know she likes (typically fruit or pasta or meatballs/loaf) on the plate with the new food, but she'll just eat the thing she likes and then throw her plate on the floor with the stuff she doesn't want to try. I understand that part of this is the developmentally normal boundary testing that they do at this age, but I've come up short trying to get her to try new foods that I really think she would enjoy based on the things that she does eat. I'm not super thrilled about the &quot;you eat this or you eat nothing&quot; method that I've had recommended to me, especially since she always seems to choose the latter when we resort to it.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","sleep","sleep-training"],"owner":{"account_id":11741257,"reputation":21,"user_id":51599,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/1bceb3284f8de4a29d71f77d10e177c6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Ct2911","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/51599/ct2911"},"is_answered":false,"view_count":142,"closed_date":1730030051,"answer_count":0,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1730030003,"creation_date":1729804353,"last_edit_date":1730030003,"question_id":44383,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44383/toddler-2-1-2-year-old-boy-won-t-sleep-without-mum-or-dad-in-his-bedroom","closed_reason":"Duplicate","title":"Toddler (2 1/2 year old boy) won’t sleep without mum or dad in his bedroom","body":"<p>Wondering if anyone has a solution for a phase we are going through with our 2 1/2 year old son. Since before he was 2, our son has had some real issues with sleeping alone in his room.</p>\n<p>He has a pretty typical routine where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dinner around 6-6.30pm</li>\n<li>Playtime 7.30-7pm</li>\n<li>Bath time 7-7.30pm</li>\n<li>7.30-8pm PJs on and read a book.</li>\n<li>8-8.30pm Milk in cot to fall asleep.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The problem we are facing is that as soon we finish the book, he immediately asks for one of us to sit next to his bed or on the double bed we have in his room as he knows we are about to put him into his cot for bed time. You can actively see the anxiety on his face and hear it in his voice as soon as he knows he has to sleep and that we might leave.</p>\n<p>The only way we can get him to sleep now is by lying in the bed in his room till he falls asleep, we then leave to go into our bedroom.</p>\n<p>He’ll then wake up at midnight crying out and we’ll have to go in and stroke his head (we normally wait in case he falls back to sleep but he never does) until he falls asleep and then leave to go back to our bedroom.</p>\n<p>The next wake up will be 3am and he then cries out even louder and this wake up he needs us to stay in the room with him otherwise he won’t go back to sleep. Both these wake ups are like clockwork and require the same method to get him back to sleep.</p>\n<p>We’ve tried all the methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>I’ll be right back - extending the returns more and more. This resulted in him becoming more worked up each time it got longer and longer.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Cry it out - walk out after saying good night and cuddles etc. we left him for over an hour crying and calling out, jumping in his cot, till we finally went in to comfort him. This was an awful experience for all of us and I don’t know if we could stomach that again.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Edging closer to the door each night. Similar to the &quot;I’ll be right back&quot; approach, he just got more anxious each time we got closer to the door.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Sleeping on the floor/bed and moving that closer to door. Same result as when we sat on the floor moving closer to the door, he would just get more anxious the closer we got.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If anyone has gone through this before, it would be great to hear how you dealt with this. Even to hear that you ended up just staying in their room and the phase passed, I think hearing how long it took would give us some peace knowing there is light at the end of the tunnel and knowing how long that realistically could be.</p>\n<p>Thank you community!</p>\n<p><strong>Dad</strong></p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","middle-childhood","friends"],"owner":{"account_id":1024210,"reputation":1651,"user_id":7062,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/439c5c978b2286c839ba825c59d4db6e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ivana","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7062/ivana"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":447,"accepted_answer_id":44385,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1729991257,"creation_date":1729808384,"question_id":44384,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44384/child-accuses-friend-of-stealing","title":"Child accuses friend of stealing","body":"<p>My child had some cash in their jacket pocket at school and it was gone when he got home. He thinks maybe one of his friends stole it and wants to confront him.</p>\n<p>Realistically, anyone could have stolen it. If he accuses his friend it will hurt his friends feelings.\nWhile this is something we will talk about, I'm not sure we should talk about it some more and then leave it to him to decide what to do. In this case i plan to tell him to discuss the disappeared money with his class or at least with a group of friends without accusing anyone in particular. Is this the right course of action?</p>\n<p>On one hand it feels wrong to disregard his hunch and to tell him to keep his suspicions to himself, even when it will bother him a lot. On the other hand, he has no proof and an\naccusation like this could ruin their friendship.\nThey are only 11.</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","language-development","speech","autism"],"owner":{"account_id":36995900,"reputation":9,"user_id":51764,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/32ec23a799114ccaadad670c56a580c8?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Vittoria Fabbri","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/51764/vittoria-fabbri"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":542,"answer_count":1,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1753794055,"creation_date":1730114467,"last_edit_date":1730811588,"question_id":44388,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44388/daughter-might-be-showing-signs-of-asd","title":"Daughter might be showing signs of ASD?","body":"<p>I have a lovely DD who today just turned 21 months and I am pretty sure she has some ASD traits.</p>\n<p>Positive</p>\n<ol>\n<li>She can say up to 100 words.</li>\n<li>She can do pointing, waves and claps, and pretend playing.</li>\n<li>She has always brought us toys / books etc, follow commands to an extent like &quot;it's bath time.&quot;</li>\n<li>She is also a good sleeper, she can self settle after the bottle.</li>\n<li>She imitates everything.</li>\n<li>She has good joint attention and always look where I am pointing.</li>\n<li>She can put two words together.</li>\n<li>She is very engaging and saying hello and smiling to people who engage with her.</li>\n<li>She can identify animals on books and tv correctly .</li>\n<li>She understands simple commands like &quot;Time to go out,&quot; &quot;bath time,&quot; &quot;get your shoes,&quot; &quot;shut the door,&quot; &quot;pick it up,&quot; or &quot;get me baby dolly&quot;</li>\n<li>She can tell me when she does a &quot;poo&quot;</li>\n<li>She can answer the questions &quot;What’s that?&quot; and &quot;Where is dolly?&quot;</li>\n<li>She is very affectionate.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Reason to worry:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>She tenses up and tippy toes when excited since she was 14 months.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>She rotates her wrists and ankles when excited, always done. It’s what worries me most.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Occasionally she does side glancing when turning her head to look at something behind her shoulders.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>She doesn’t answer simple questions like &quot;Do you want milk?&quot;</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>She has an extraordinary memory.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>My HV (health visitor, a nurse who comes to check up on the child), and doctor don’t see a reason to assess her. Now I understand that would be close to impossible getting a diagnosis or assessment based on this but was wondering if any of your kids had this presentation .\nMany thanks ☺️</p>\n"},{"tags":["toddler","eating"],"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":576,"answer_count":4,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1731474500,"creation_date":1730136162,"question_id":44389,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44389/how-to-deal-with-an-older-toddler-who-refuses-to-eat-anything-but-cheerios","title":"How to deal with an older toddler who refuses to eat anything but cheerios?","body":"<p>Our three year old is developing another bad habit - he absolutely refuses to eat anything except bowl after bowl of cheerios!</p>\n<p>...Well okay it's not that bad - he will sometimes try new foods he is curious about, and he will gladly scarf down yogurt pouches and usually goes for nutrigrain bars too.</p>\n<p>But he gets extremely upset if we don't constantly keep a little bowl of cheerios at the ready for him - tugging on our finger to beg us for them (he is speech delayed) and even throwing a fit if we refuse to give him any (and refusing to eat anything else).</p>\n<p>He is, for the <em>most</em> part, doing okay on his nutrition, but he used to eat a much more varied number of foods, and I'm worried about him developing a bad habit of only ever filling up on cheerios.</p>\n<p>What should we do?</p>\n"},{"tags":["teen"],"owner":{"account_id":3152636,"reputation":21,"user_id":7604,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/1feb3fe52af4385c90337294aad2d45b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Srav","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7604/srav"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":303,"answer_count":1,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1730464691,"creation_date":1730148024,"last_edit_date":1730383880,"question_id":44390,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44390/stressed-out-highschooler","title":"Stressed out highschooler","body":"<p>I have an amazing son; he is a very good person, likes helping people, patient, sweet and very hard working. However, he doesn't know when to stop trying and ask for help. He is enrolled in a highly competitive STEM-magnet program in high school and he is usually stressed out.</p>\n<p>With lots of school work, projects, and college prep etc., he can't go to bed early, and when he tries to go to bed, it takes him a lot of time to fall asleep. And then he is tired the next day.</p>\n<p>Obviously, it has become a cycle and weekends aren't helping either as he needs to catch up on long-term projects/clubs from school. As a parent, I feel helpless in this situation, and have nothing to do other than encourage him that he is doing well. How are other teenagers handling this stress, their time and all the workload?</p>\n"},{"tags":["behavior","responsible"],"owner":{"account_id":1023409,"reputation":111,"user_id":51946,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/8a1af4129893a8e469e50807d290f96c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Shiva","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/51946/shiva"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":282,"closed_date":1730809730,"answer_count":1,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1730675159,"creation_date":1730443898,"last_edit_date":1730675159,"question_id":44395,"link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/questions/44395/how-to-deal-with-difficult-parents-who-takes-things-for-granted-and-wont-take-a","closed_reason":"Not suitable for this site","title":"How to deal with difficult parents who takes things for granted and won&#39;t take accountability for student&#39;s lack of discipline?","body":"<p>By profession, I am a young computer engineer who has agreed to provide tutoring for computer science and AI to a girl studying in higher secondary school in India. She is from a family in the neighborhood, whose parents are somewhat good neighborhood friends of my parents. I gave her a strict day and time for her tutoring sessions which is Friday at 7 PM. However, she tends to skip the sessions occasionally, or she delays joining the sessions, attending very late. Over that, her parents expect me to be available any time to teach her because they reside very near to me and they expect that their daughter can come anytime to me and I will teach her, or make up for her missed sessions, etc.</p>\n<p>When it comes to her other sessions, if she has another tutoring session with someone else that conflicts with my day and time (which is again on Friday at 7 PM), then her parents allow her to skip my classes and join that other tutoring session instead. After my session is skipped, her parents also expect that I make up for the session that she misses and manage to get me to agree by speaking in a way that's very clever and persuades me to allow it.</p>\n<p>Yesterday, I talked with her father (who is the main person who takes my time, efforts and availability for granted). I clearly and boldly told him that I won't tolerate the lack of discipline and punctuality of his daughter's attendance, and I won't be making up for the sessions that she misses from her end. I also requested him not to take my classes for granted and give equal importance to my tutoring sessions, just like they give for the other tutors.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, I told him that any additional skipping of tutoring sessions and I won't be able to make up for her because the burden keeps increasing. The multiple delays means that all the chapters that were supposed to be finished on time are now being pushed later and they will pile up and cause huge burden just before the final exams.</p>\n<p>But her father instead started to get defensive about his daughter. He also started to question me on why I did not inform about his daughter's lack of discipline and punctuality before. I was stunned. Being her father, doesn't he know that his daughter is not being disciplined and punctual in attending my tutoring sessions? Is it not his responsibility as her parent? In fact, he is the one who gave her the freedom and flexibility to come to my tutoring sessions at any time she wants because she resides nearby, ruining all her discipline and sense of commitment to attend the sessions sincerely and study.</p>\n<p>I somehow think that my efforts, time and availability are taken for granted. I am thinking that her father and mother think that I will do anything that they say and adapt according to their requests. And when I am approaching her parents regarding the lack of discipline and punctuality of their daughter, then her parents are going on the defensive instead of trying to come up with a solution.</p>\n<p>How do I make the parents understand this situation without being too bold or rude?</p>\n"}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":286}